1
00:00:00,985 --> 00:00:02,440
[SQUEAK]

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:03,895
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,895 --> 00:00:07,290
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:12,940 --> 00:00:16,440
SARA BEERY: So today, we're moving on

5
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:19,000
from generative models, and we're

6
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:22,360
going to start talking about out-of-distribution

7
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:24,720
generalization.

8
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:28,080
And this is, I think, one of the biggest open challenges

9
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:31,080
for modern machine learning models, the ability

10
00:00:31,080 --> 00:00:34,840
to generalize to data that is not explicitly covered

11
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:37,920
within the training distribution.

12
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:43,160
And so, just a bit of motivation here.

13
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:45,960
We've seen a lot of success in machine learning, right?

14
00:00:45,960 --> 00:00:50,760
We have incredible models for image classification.

15
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:54,020
This is a aggregate of the ImageNet data set.

16
00:00:54,020 --> 00:00:56,880
I would argue that, in many ways,

17
00:00:56,880 --> 00:00:59,400
things like simple categorization, the types

18
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:03,130
of categorization we often try to do on data

19
00:01:03,130 --> 00:01:06,290
sets like ImageNet is pretty much solved.

20
00:01:06,290 --> 00:01:08,570
There are, of course, a few corner

21
00:01:08,570 --> 00:01:12,770
cases that are pretty difficult, or sometimes, even erroneously

22
00:01:12,770 --> 00:01:15,650
labeled or ambiguous that are still hard for modern models

23
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:19,410
within ImageNet, but mostly, it's a solved problem.

24
00:01:19,410 --> 00:01:22,830
We've seen pretty great success on image generation.

25
00:01:22,830 --> 00:01:25,410
We've been talking about that the last couple of days.

26
00:01:25,410 --> 00:01:29,050
Strategy games, things like machine translation,

27
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:30,570
robotic manipulation, we're starting

28
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:34,130
to see success in all of these.

29
00:01:34,130 --> 00:01:38,770
But then there are a lot of cases where machine learning

30
00:01:38,770 --> 00:01:40,930
still isn't very successful.

31
00:01:40,930 --> 00:01:43,310
And sometimes, it can be surprising.

32
00:01:43,310 --> 00:01:47,690
So I think one interesting example here

33
00:01:47,690 --> 00:01:50,790
is in the space of self-driving cars.

34
00:01:50,790 --> 00:01:54,230
So we are starting to see self-driving cars be deployed.

35
00:01:54,230 --> 00:01:56,530
I think Waymo in particular is starting

36
00:01:56,530 --> 00:02:00,340
to actually have fleets deployed in many different areas.

37
00:02:00,340 --> 00:02:03,500
And of course, there's complicated dynamics

38
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,540
around why we're not seeing self-driving cars at scale.

39
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:09,620
Regulation plays a huge role in that.

40
00:02:09,620 --> 00:02:11,940
But I think one of the main reasons

41
00:02:11,940 --> 00:02:15,020
is because we really don't have a good mechanism to guarantee

42
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:18,180
the robustness of these models.

43
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:20,220
We can't guarantee that they are not

44
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:23,980
going to make potentially harmful mistakes.

45
00:02:23,980 --> 00:02:26,500
And this really begs the question of,

46
00:02:26,500 --> 00:02:28,780
are these machine learning systems really ready

47
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:30,700
for the real world?

48
00:02:30,700 --> 00:02:36,340
And what dimensions of deployment in the real world

49
00:02:36,340 --> 00:02:37,500
are we still missing?

50
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:39,220
What do we need to be focusing on

51
00:02:39,220 --> 00:02:41,980
to be able to actually do this effectively?

52
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:47,460
And I do think there's an interesting point here,

53
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:50,020
which is you could argue that a lot of machine learning

54
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:52,920
is actually actively being deployed in the real world.

55
00:02:52,920 --> 00:02:56,940
I think the GPT models is a great example of that.

56
00:02:56,940 --> 00:03:00,960
People are using AI more and more day to day,

57
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:03,000
but if I'm trying to characterize

58
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:05,680
the types of AI that are ready for deployment

59
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:09,760
versus the types of AI that, even if we try to deploy them,

60
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:12,200
we invest billions of dollars into figuring out

61
00:03:12,200 --> 00:03:15,600
how to make them deployable, we're still not seeing

62
00:03:15,600 --> 00:03:19,000
that progress or actually really seeing it happen,

63
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:22,600
I think it comes down to risk.

64
00:03:22,600 --> 00:03:26,160
A lot of the cases where we see AI models deployed

65
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:29,400
are what I would characterize as high precision

66
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:31,020
human verification systems.

67
00:03:31,020 --> 00:03:35,280
So you need the model to be good enough to be useful,

68
00:03:35,280 --> 00:03:37,480
so it should work most of the time,

69
00:03:37,480 --> 00:03:39,400
but you also have a human verifier

70
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,520
in the loop that is making corrections

71
00:03:42,520 --> 00:03:44,580
when something's not right.

72
00:03:44,580 --> 00:03:46,400
So you have this verification loop

73
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:49,040
that's always happening with human intelligence.

74
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:50,680
ChatGPT is a great example of this.

75
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:52,020
You prompt it with something.

76
00:03:52,020 --> 00:03:53,620
You get a result. Maybe it's useful.

77
00:03:53,620 --> 00:03:54,980
You probably edit it.

78
00:03:54,980 --> 00:03:57,810
You probably interact with it in some way.

79
00:03:57,810 --> 00:04:00,383
If it's just absolute trash, maybe you giggle about it,

80
00:04:00,383 --> 00:04:02,050
you share it with your friends, and then

81
00:04:02,050 --> 00:04:04,490
you prompt it with something completely different.

82
00:04:04,490 --> 00:04:07,730
So there's this human brain that's in the loop,

83
00:04:07,730 --> 00:04:10,130
verifying that these things work well.

84
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:12,730
And things like hallucination aren't so much of an issue

85
00:04:12,730 --> 00:04:14,950
if a human is making the corrections themselves.

86
00:04:14,950 --> 00:04:17,209
It's when we start trusting these models

87
00:04:17,209 --> 00:04:18,890
without being human verified that we

88
00:04:18,890 --> 00:04:20,705
start running into issues.

89
00:04:20,705 --> 00:04:22,330
And I think another interesting example

90
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:25,850
of that is things like a lot of the modern machine learning

91
00:04:25,850 --> 00:04:28,210
systems that are actively deployed in health care

92
00:04:28,210 --> 00:04:31,210
are what we call decision support tools.

93
00:04:31,210 --> 00:04:34,770
So they're a tool that maybe a radiologist uses

94
00:04:34,770 --> 00:04:37,370
to try to highlight or potentially give

95
00:04:37,370 --> 00:04:43,590
some advice on what, maybe, a diagnosis could be.

96
00:04:43,590 --> 00:04:47,610
And then the human is actually liable to verify that and make

97
00:04:47,610 --> 00:04:48,610
a decision.

98
00:04:48,610 --> 00:04:51,530
And interestingly, there's been a bunch of fascinating work

99
00:04:51,530 --> 00:04:54,010
that's come out recently that really argues both sides of

100
00:04:54,010 --> 00:04:56,060
whether or not this is working.

101
00:04:56,060 --> 00:04:58,900
There's research that showed that these decision support

102
00:04:58,900 --> 00:05:03,140
tools are really great for trying to make sure that patient

103
00:05:03,140 --> 00:05:06,598
outcomes are beneficial, that doctors aren't missing things,

104
00:05:06,598 --> 00:05:08,140
and then there's conflicting research

105
00:05:08,140 --> 00:05:11,060
that explicitly says that the deployment of these decision

106
00:05:11,060 --> 00:05:14,340
support tools is actively harming patient outcomes.

107
00:05:14,340 --> 00:05:16,120
Because doctors are becoming compliant,

108
00:05:16,120 --> 00:05:18,140
they're not paying attention, and they're missing things

109
00:05:18,140 --> 00:05:20,432
in that long tail of the distribution that are actually

110
00:05:20,432 --> 00:05:22,620
quite important.

111
00:05:22,620 --> 00:05:24,300
So the jury's still out.

112
00:05:24,300 --> 00:05:27,780
And fascinatingly, if you look at the regulation around what it

113
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:29,540
actually takes to deploy decision support

114
00:05:29,540 --> 00:05:34,220
tool in a hospital, the metrics are kind of shocking.

115
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:36,520
I'm not going to quote myself on the details,

116
00:05:36,520 --> 00:05:38,020
but essentially, it's like, you have

117
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:42,740
to prove some percent accuracy on some small population

118
00:05:42,740 --> 00:05:44,900
of data.

119
00:05:44,900 --> 00:05:47,245
But it's not nearly what you would expect.

120
00:05:47,245 --> 00:05:48,620
I remember being quite surprised.

121
00:05:48,620 --> 00:05:51,780
Something like on the order of like 100 or 1,000 patients to be

122
00:05:51,780 --> 00:05:53,447
able to deploy a decision support tool.

123
00:05:53,447 --> 00:05:56,030
And you don't have to go through anything like clinical trials

124
00:05:56,030 --> 00:05:58,770
because you're not actually making the decision.

125
00:05:58,770 --> 00:06:02,590
You're just trying to support the decision of an expert.

126
00:06:02,590 --> 00:06:08,290
So, really, when we have this case,

127
00:06:08,290 --> 00:06:11,630
this is where we start to see things working well.

128
00:06:11,630 --> 00:06:14,590
It's kind of the standard machine learning setting.

129
00:06:14,590 --> 00:06:17,230
And it matches a lot of the underlying assumptions

130
00:06:17,230 --> 00:06:20,030
we make in machine learning, even theoretically,

131
00:06:20,030 --> 00:06:22,790
that the data that we are training the model on

132
00:06:22,790 --> 00:06:24,950
is going to be IID, Independently

133
00:06:24,950 --> 00:06:27,350
and Identically Distributed to the data we're

134
00:06:27,350 --> 00:06:28,810
going to use the model on.

135
00:06:28,810 --> 00:06:31,910
So you have some distribution over categories of interest,

136
00:06:31,910 --> 00:06:36,110
data, different representative visual characteristics

137
00:06:36,110 --> 00:06:38,590
within a category, and you're going to sample

138
00:06:38,590 --> 00:06:40,570
from that at random for training,

139
00:06:40,570 --> 00:06:42,270
and then you're going to do inference

140
00:06:42,270 --> 00:06:43,770
on the rest of the data.

141
00:06:43,770 --> 00:06:47,230
Training distribution equals test distribution.

142
00:06:47,230 --> 00:06:50,510
This is never true in the real world.

143
00:06:50,510 --> 00:06:54,360
So basically, there has never been

144
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:56,760
any such thing as a representative data

145
00:06:56,760 --> 00:06:59,400
set of all reality.

146
00:06:59,400 --> 00:07:03,520
And one of the reasons that this is true, even with our best

147
00:07:03,520 --> 00:07:06,680
efforts, things like these huge data sets

148
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:09,480
lie on billions of image data sets

149
00:07:09,480 --> 00:07:13,460
that try to just tackle this problem with pure scale.

150
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:16,880
We're going to train on all the data we have and hope that that

151
00:07:16,880 --> 00:07:20,040
is close to being representative and IID to the data

152
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:21,960
we want to use the models on.

153
00:07:21,960 --> 00:07:25,160
It still doesn't work because the world is changing.

154
00:07:25,160 --> 00:07:28,160
So if you capture all the data today, it's

155
00:07:28,160 --> 00:07:30,040
not going to necessarily be representative

156
00:07:30,040 --> 00:07:31,820
of a month from now, a week from now.

157
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:34,520
Things happen, things change.

158
00:07:34,520 --> 00:07:37,960
And so we often have something like this.

159
00:07:37,960 --> 00:07:40,760
There's some fundamental difference between the training

160
00:07:40,760 --> 00:07:45,400
and test distribution once we move to a deployment setting.

161
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:47,422
And so here, maybe some version of that

162
00:07:47,422 --> 00:07:48,880
would be something like, all right,

163
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:51,060
we've trained a model on Cambridge.

164
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:52,760
So this is this data center.

165
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:55,060
And now, we're trying to use that model

166
00:07:55,060 --> 00:07:56,160
in a different season.

167
00:07:56,160 --> 00:07:59,140
Now there's snow on the ground and the model starts to fail.

168
00:07:59,140 --> 00:08:00,598
And there's a lot of different ways

169
00:08:00,598 --> 00:08:03,420
that we can think about how those distributions can change.

170
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:06,980
The data itself can become perturbed.

171
00:08:06,980 --> 00:08:09,400
That could be adversarially.

172
00:08:09,400 --> 00:08:11,700
We'll talk about that in a bit.

173
00:08:11,700 --> 00:08:14,540
But it also can be simple things like,

174
00:08:14,540 --> 00:08:20,300
h, the manufacturer of the cameras for Google Street View

175
00:08:20,300 --> 00:08:22,788
cars made an update to their system,

176
00:08:22,788 --> 00:08:24,580
and now there's some statistical difference

177
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,180
in terms of the sensor itself.

178
00:08:27,180 --> 00:08:29,440
You can get a different label distribution.

179
00:08:29,440 --> 00:08:34,840
So maybe the set of categories of interest has changed.

180
00:08:34,840 --> 00:08:36,460
You end up with something that used

181
00:08:36,460 --> 00:08:39,220
to be really rare becoming much more common,

182
00:08:39,220 --> 00:08:42,580
or something just disappears entirely.

183
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:46,140
And then there can be lots of other types of shifts.

184
00:08:46,140 --> 00:08:50,570
These can be shifts in the size of the sequences or the graphs

185
00:08:50,570 --> 00:08:53,830
you're modeling, weather, geospatial shifts

186
00:08:53,830 --> 00:08:55,750
from different countries or cities.

187
00:08:55,750 --> 00:08:56,730
Source of measurement.

188
00:08:56,730 --> 00:08:59,230
That's like the sensor example I said.

189
00:08:59,230 --> 00:09:02,390
So what actually goes wrong when we have these distribution

190
00:09:02,390 --> 00:09:04,030
differences?

191
00:09:04,030 --> 00:09:06,553
So I thought this was a cute example.

192
00:09:06,553 --> 00:09:08,470
It's a little hard to read, actually, probably

193
00:09:08,470 --> 00:09:11,550
because I took a screenshot.

194
00:09:11,550 --> 00:09:17,390
So this is a tweet by hardmaru that was basically talking about

195
00:09:17,390 --> 00:09:21,070
essentially, there was a failure mode in Roombas

196
00:09:21,070 --> 00:09:24,630
where they kept rolling over dog poop

197
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:27,350
and then smearing poop all over people's houses.

198
00:09:27,350 --> 00:09:32,190
And so in order to try to avoid this happening, they went out

199
00:09:32,190 --> 00:09:35,750
and they collected a very large, diverse data set of dog poop.

200
00:09:35,750 --> 00:09:38,650
So I'm sure that was someone's like research internship.

201
00:09:42,590 --> 00:09:45,767
So they're talking about, oh, OK, in order to do this right,

202
00:09:45,767 --> 00:09:47,350
the way that we're going to generalize

203
00:09:47,350 --> 00:09:50,000
to of new out of distribution thing, which is like dog poop

204
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,500
that we didn't expect to be on the floor in someone's house,

205
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:54,458
we're going to collect this representative data

206
00:09:54,458 --> 00:09:55,200
set of dog poop.

207
00:09:55,200 --> 00:09:58,420
And then the response was from Dmitry.

208
00:09:58,420 --> 00:10:01,280
And he said, man, I wish they'd also created a diverse data

209
00:10:01,280 --> 00:10:04,940
set of rugs so it didn't confuse black stripes with cliffs

210
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:07,240
and I could finally get my whole house cleaned.

211
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:11,080
So this is pointing out you can collect data

212
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:13,880
for the distribution shift you do anticipate,

213
00:10:13,880 --> 00:10:16,860
but there's always going to be things you don't anticipate.

214
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:19,440
And then you end up with Dmitry, who bought a Roomba

215
00:10:19,440 --> 00:10:22,640
and basically gets one tiny square of his rug cleaned

216
00:10:22,640 --> 00:10:25,760
a million times.

217
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:29,460
So this is a silly, silly example,

218
00:10:29,460 --> 00:10:33,360
but the point here is really that we cannot possibly

219
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:36,280
characterize and anticipate every way that the world is

220
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:37,960
going to change.

221
00:10:37,960 --> 00:10:41,300
And so this idea that we can collect representative,

222
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:43,800
sufficient data to just ensure that where

223
00:10:43,800 --> 00:10:46,200
we're going to deploy the model is IID from what we train

224
00:10:46,200 --> 00:10:48,930
the data on is quite difficult.

225
00:10:48,930 --> 00:10:52,770
This is reflected in quite concrete problems when you're

226
00:10:52,770 --> 00:10:55,010
talking about AI safety, the safety of things that

227
00:10:55,010 --> 00:10:57,150
are deployed, particularly in high risk scenarios,

228
00:10:57,150 --> 00:11:00,290
things like medicine or self-driving cars.

229
00:11:00,290 --> 00:11:03,370
This was a paper that was explicitly characterizing

230
00:11:03,370 --> 00:11:06,050
what they thought were the largest problems in AI safety,

231
00:11:06,050 --> 00:11:10,730
and robustness to distributional change was one of them.

232
00:11:10,730 --> 00:11:12,810
There was this really interesting workshop

233
00:11:12,810 --> 00:11:15,370
that happened back in 2022 at Princeton

234
00:11:15,370 --> 00:11:17,050
that they called The Reproducibility

235
00:11:17,050 --> 00:11:20,050
Crisis in Machine Learning-Based Science.

236
00:11:20,050 --> 00:11:23,530
They looked at about 1,000 different papers across a huge

237
00:11:23,530 --> 00:11:25,570
diversity of scientific disciplines,

238
00:11:25,570 --> 00:11:29,690
and they essentially found that they diagnosed a bunch

239
00:11:29,690 --> 00:11:33,410
of different potential reasons why they weren't actually seeing

240
00:11:33,410 --> 00:11:38,930
reproducibility or generalizability in the papers

241
00:11:38,930 --> 00:11:40,810
that they were analyzing.

242
00:11:40,810 --> 00:11:47,790
And one of the biggest failure modes here was data poisoning.

243
00:11:47,790 --> 00:11:51,750
So basically, the way that they constructed the evaluation

244
00:11:51,750 --> 00:11:54,590
for these machine learning systems was, in some way,

245
00:11:54,590 --> 00:11:58,070
artificially IID in a way that wasn't representative

246
00:11:58,070 --> 00:11:59,602
of reality.

247
00:11:59,602 --> 00:12:01,810
And this is something that I actually run into a lot.

248
00:12:01,810 --> 00:12:06,870
I review a lot of papers for my other half

249
00:12:06,870 --> 00:12:10,670
discipline, which is ecology or environmental science.

250
00:12:10,670 --> 00:12:13,390
And in those papers, often, what I

251
00:12:13,390 --> 00:12:16,110
see is a mismatch between the data

252
00:12:16,110 --> 00:12:19,230
that people are developing and training and evaluating

253
00:12:19,230 --> 00:12:23,030
machine learning methods on and the claims that they're making.

254
00:12:23,030 --> 00:12:26,830
So it's fine to have a limited data set,

255
00:12:26,830 --> 00:12:28,350
but then you have to understand what

256
00:12:28,350 --> 00:12:30,710
that means about what you can claim the effectiveness

257
00:12:30,710 --> 00:12:32,590
of the model is going to do.

258
00:12:32,590 --> 00:12:36,470
If you have built a system that identifies

259
00:12:36,470 --> 00:12:39,985
all of the individual macaques in a national park,

260
00:12:39,985 --> 00:12:42,110
you can say something reasonable about your ability

261
00:12:42,110 --> 00:12:45,780
to monitor macaques in that national park going forward,

262
00:12:45,780 --> 00:12:47,840
as long as you've evaluated your ability

263
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:50,080
to understand the changes in time

264
00:12:50,080 --> 00:12:51,420
for those individual macaques.

265
00:12:51,420 --> 00:12:53,920
And so somehow, the way you evaluate these models

266
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:56,862
needs to be representative of the actual deployment use.

267
00:12:56,862 --> 00:12:59,320
And I think this takes a lot of really careful construction

268
00:12:59,320 --> 00:12:59,780
and thought.

269
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:02,360
Actually, the way we think about designing evaluation systems

270
00:13:02,360 --> 00:13:05,760
for machine learning models is really non-trivial.

271
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:08,840
And if you use any of off-the-shelf tool, things

272
00:13:08,840 --> 00:13:11,080
like Roboflow, it just automatically

273
00:13:11,080 --> 00:13:13,020
assumes that random splitting is fine

274
00:13:13,020 --> 00:13:16,048
and you get, often, very falsely high expectations

275
00:13:16,048 --> 00:13:17,840
in terms of the performance of your machine

276
00:13:17,840 --> 00:13:21,240
learning model because you've got data poisoning, essentially,

277
00:13:21,240 --> 00:13:24,760
strong, spatial, temporal, or contextual correlations

278
00:13:24,760 --> 00:13:26,520
in your training and test data that

279
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:30,840
make the model think it's doing well when it's actually not.

280
00:13:30,840 --> 00:13:35,680
So for today, we're going to be talking first about specifically

281
00:13:35,680 --> 00:13:38,960
adversarial examples and thinking

282
00:13:38,960 --> 00:13:43,960
about how you might train to address adversarial examples.

283
00:13:43,960 --> 00:13:46,610
So this is small perturbations, ways

284
00:13:46,610 --> 00:13:49,290
that you think about robustness on the level of individual data

285
00:13:49,290 --> 00:13:50,275
points.

286
00:13:50,275 --> 00:13:51,650
And then, after that, we're going

287
00:13:51,650 --> 00:13:53,317
to be talking about distribution shifts.

288
00:13:53,317 --> 00:13:55,130
So actually, just these large-scale strips

289
00:13:55,130 --> 00:14:00,330
from entire distributions of data and some simple

290
00:14:00,330 --> 00:14:02,730
mechanisms that people use to try to address them.

291
00:14:02,730 --> 00:14:04,730
Though I do want to point out that both of these

292
00:14:04,730 --> 00:14:07,530
are pretty active, open areas of research.

293
00:14:07,530 --> 00:14:09,090
So I can talk about progress, but we

294
00:14:09,090 --> 00:14:12,090
don't have solutions for these.

295
00:14:12,090 --> 00:14:17,450
So first, if we're talking about adversarial examples,

296
00:14:17,450 --> 00:14:21,210
assume we have a simple machine learning classifier right.

297
00:14:21,210 --> 00:14:23,450
We have this image of a pig.

298
00:14:23,450 --> 00:14:25,250
The model says, yep, that's a pig.

299
00:14:25,250 --> 00:14:27,210
91% confidence.

300
00:14:27,210 --> 00:14:29,770
What an adversarial example does is

301
00:14:29,770 --> 00:14:36,430
it adds some very small amount of random-- or not random,

302
00:14:36,430 --> 00:14:41,690
but what looks like random noise to this image.

303
00:14:41,690 --> 00:14:49,140
And you can see it's 0.005 times of this thing that looks random.

304
00:14:49,140 --> 00:14:52,060
And so when you're adding this noise, which is explicitly not

305
00:14:52,060 --> 00:14:56,500
random, it's actually optimized to be maximally adversarial.

306
00:14:56,500 --> 00:14:59,380
You can actually change the prediction of a model

307
00:14:59,380 --> 00:15:01,580
to be something completely different, maybe even

308
00:15:01,580 --> 00:15:06,780
with higher confidence than the initial prediction,

309
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:10,180
even though, to me, this image looks identical.

310
00:15:10,180 --> 00:15:13,220
I can see no actual visible difference

311
00:15:13,220 --> 00:15:15,700
between the two images.

312
00:15:15,700 --> 00:15:19,940
And so this points to the brittleness within, often,

313
00:15:19,940 --> 00:15:21,398
machine learning models.

314
00:15:21,398 --> 00:15:22,940
They can be mostly accurate, but they

315
00:15:22,940 --> 00:15:27,060
can be really brittle to things that a human visual system is

316
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:28,780
not brittle to at all.

317
00:15:28,780 --> 00:15:30,160
And this is a big challenge.

318
00:15:30,160 --> 00:15:34,020
So you can imagine that if you could

319
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:36,190
do this of adversarial attack in something

320
00:15:36,190 --> 00:15:37,940
like self-driving cars, you could actually

321
00:15:37,940 --> 00:15:41,870
get models to explicitly change their decisions about street

322
00:15:41,870 --> 00:15:44,390
signs.

323
00:15:44,390 --> 00:15:51,510
And that would require that you're able to actually generate

324
00:15:51,510 --> 00:15:53,262
something that, in physical reality,

325
00:15:53,262 --> 00:15:54,970
is not just related to individual images.

326
00:15:54,970 --> 00:15:56,450
It's actually in physical reality.

327
00:15:56,450 --> 00:15:57,570
But that can also be done.

328
00:15:57,570 --> 00:15:59,640
So let's see, there's a--

329
00:15:59,640 --> 00:16:02,210
I have a movie here, but I wonder if it'll play.

330
00:16:07,870 --> 00:16:09,630
Yeah, there we go.

331
00:16:09,630 --> 00:16:10,730
So this is an example.

332
00:16:10,730 --> 00:16:12,170
So here's a classifier, right?

333
00:16:12,170 --> 00:16:13,530
It's classifying banana.

334
00:16:13,530 --> 00:16:16,230
If you put a toaster in there, it

335
00:16:16,230 --> 00:16:18,150
doesn't change the prediction.

336
00:16:18,150 --> 00:16:20,870
But if you put this very specially constructed

337
00:16:20,870 --> 00:16:25,430
adversarial sticker in there, it really convinces the model

338
00:16:25,430 --> 00:16:27,470
that it is, in fact, a toaster.

339
00:16:27,470 --> 00:16:31,230
So if we watch that again.

340
00:16:31,230 --> 00:16:33,950
So the interesting thing here is that I

341
00:16:33,950 --> 00:16:35,870
would argue from a classification standpoint,

342
00:16:35,870 --> 00:16:37,090
this is really ambiguous.

343
00:16:37,090 --> 00:16:40,130
You would expect a model would actually maybe even reasonably

344
00:16:40,130 --> 00:16:40,910
predict toaster.

345
00:16:40,910 --> 00:16:42,890
They're about the same size, whatever.

346
00:16:42,890 --> 00:16:47,850
Whereas here, this looks nothing really like a toaster to us.

347
00:16:47,850 --> 00:16:51,530
And so the interesting thing here

348
00:16:51,530 --> 00:16:54,330
is that this demonstrates that it's not

349
00:16:54,330 --> 00:16:56,447
something about high-level image characteristics,

350
00:16:56,447 --> 00:16:58,530
that you can actually really convince these models

351
00:16:58,530 --> 00:17:02,330
or trick these models even in physical reality.

352
00:17:02,330 --> 00:17:05,930
And there are fun examples of this, even in 3D.

353
00:17:05,930 --> 00:17:11,170
So this is an adversarial 3D printed example of a turtle that

354
00:17:11,170 --> 00:17:14,490
most image classification models will categorize very confidently

355
00:17:14,490 --> 00:17:17,089
as a rifle.

356
00:17:17,089 --> 00:17:20,569
And you can actually show, there's a fun video

357
00:17:20,569 --> 00:17:22,130
if you go look this up.

358
00:17:22,130 --> 00:17:23,069
Oh, here we go.

359
00:17:23,069 --> 00:17:24,369
Perfect.

360
00:17:24,369 --> 00:17:26,930
Yeah, so it stays-- the prediction stays rifle,

361
00:17:26,930 --> 00:17:28,270
even if you move it around.

362
00:17:28,270 --> 00:17:30,770
It exists in 3D.

363
00:17:30,770 --> 00:17:35,130
And all you're doing is changing the texture.

364
00:17:35,130 --> 00:17:40,620
And they basically showed that their 3D turtle was really

365
00:17:40,620 --> 00:17:43,580
consistently predicted as either rifle or sometimes as something

366
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:46,525
else, but it was never actually categorized as a turtle.

367
00:17:46,525 --> 00:17:48,900
And you can do this type of adversarial attack for speech

368
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:50,260
as well.

369
00:17:50,260 --> 00:17:55,180
You can add just a little bit of very explicitly targeted noise

370
00:17:55,180 --> 00:17:58,420
in a way that perceptually, if we listened to these, or even

371
00:17:58,420 --> 00:18:00,020
visually, within the waveform, looks

372
00:18:00,020 --> 00:18:02,000
like it's made very, very little difference,

373
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:03,340
and it will actually--

374
00:18:03,340 --> 00:18:05,940
the model will predict that the translation of that speech

375
00:18:05,940 --> 00:18:07,780
is completely different.

376
00:18:07,780 --> 00:18:10,780
So what does this mean about our models?

377
00:18:10,780 --> 00:18:11,680
Are they useless?

378
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:15,180
What about them is so brittle?

379
00:18:15,180 --> 00:18:16,720
Why does this happen.

380
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:18,700
And can you prevent it?

381
00:18:18,700 --> 00:18:22,247
So a bit of a history of adversarial examples.

382
00:18:22,247 --> 00:18:24,580
So this has been something that people have been looking

383
00:18:24,580 --> 00:18:26,180
at for quite a long time.

384
00:18:26,180 --> 00:18:29,900
Some of the first works in this space were from back in 2004.

385
00:18:29,900 --> 00:18:34,980
And there they were looking at adversarial examples for things

386
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:38,090
like simple linear classifiers.

387
00:18:38,090 --> 00:18:40,950
And one of the original motivating examples

388
00:18:40,950 --> 00:18:43,190
was a spam filter.

389
00:18:43,190 --> 00:18:45,790
So if you tried to build some classifier that

390
00:18:45,790 --> 00:18:47,990
tried to categorize whether an email was spam

391
00:18:47,990 --> 00:18:51,830
or not, could you, basically, from a red team, blue team

392
00:18:51,830 --> 00:18:54,150
perspective, try to actually build

393
00:18:54,150 --> 00:18:58,750
emails that would explicitly get through your spam filter?

394
00:18:58,750 --> 00:19:02,630
And this has only gone on from there.

395
00:19:02,630 --> 00:19:04,450
There's been a significant amount of work.

396
00:19:04,450 --> 00:19:07,230
This was a review back from even 2018.

397
00:19:07,230 --> 00:19:09,990
But now, particularly maybe in the context

398
00:19:09,990 --> 00:19:13,510
of today being voting day in the United States,

399
00:19:13,510 --> 00:19:15,230
there's a lot of work in this type

400
00:19:15,230 --> 00:19:20,950
of adversarial dimension looking at misinformation filters now.

401
00:19:20,950 --> 00:19:24,630
So how do you-- how do you try to detect and categorize

402
00:19:24,630 --> 00:19:29,530
active misinformation or deepfakes on the internet,

403
00:19:29,530 --> 00:19:33,350
on social media platforms, and then the active antagonism

404
00:19:33,350 --> 00:19:36,190
of trying to actually develop deepfakes

405
00:19:36,190 --> 00:19:39,680
that are undetectable by these algorithms.

406
00:19:39,680 --> 00:19:43,840
So it's important and it's difficult.

407
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:45,855
So we'll talk about how do you actually do this,

408
00:19:45,855 --> 00:19:47,480
in maybe the simplest sense, how do you

409
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:49,920
create an adversarial example.

410
00:19:49,920 --> 00:19:53,260
So at first, we're going to assume, at least for now,

411
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:55,500
that we have some fixed machine learning model.

412
00:19:55,500 --> 00:19:59,200
And so now, what we're doing is we're

413
00:19:59,200 --> 00:20:03,040
actually optimizing over data related to that model.

414
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:06,120
So what we want to do is we want to find a small perturbation

415
00:20:06,120 --> 00:20:09,540
to a given image that doesn't change the meaning to a human,

416
00:20:09,540 --> 00:20:12,760
but does change the meaning to a machine learning model.

417
00:20:12,760 --> 00:20:17,080
And so the model is going to output some probability of given

418
00:20:17,080 --> 00:20:19,480
theta, of some given machine learning

419
00:20:19,480 --> 00:20:24,040
model weights and architecture, some probability of y

420
00:20:24,040 --> 00:20:26,120
the category given x.

421
00:20:26,120 --> 00:20:30,240
We might take some softmax to get that prediction.

422
00:20:30,240 --> 00:20:34,040
So what an adversarial example does is instead,

423
00:20:34,040 --> 00:20:38,650
now, you try to solve this optimization problem that

424
00:20:38,650 --> 00:20:45,010
finds the maximum over all possible small delta, so

425
00:20:45,010 --> 00:20:50,650
small changes to x, that you now instead predict

426
00:20:50,650 --> 00:20:53,310
a different category.

427
00:20:53,310 --> 00:20:56,530
So some target category that is not the original category.

428
00:20:56,530 --> 00:20:58,150
And you can do this for any category.

429
00:20:58,150 --> 00:21:00,210
You can find the noise that would make this pig look most

430
00:21:00,210 --> 00:21:01,710
like an airliner, but you could also

431
00:21:01,710 --> 00:21:03,870
find the noise that made this pig look most like,

432
00:21:03,870 --> 00:21:06,490
I don't know, your friend Bob.

433
00:21:06,490 --> 00:21:08,982
And so you pick some wrong class and then

434
00:21:08,982 --> 00:21:10,690
you have some given input image, and then

435
00:21:10,690 --> 00:21:15,010
you find a small perturbation that actually maximizes this.

436
00:21:15,010 --> 00:21:17,290
And this will be within the constraints of some set

437
00:21:17,290 --> 00:21:22,610
of, quote unquote, "allowed perturbations."

438
00:21:22,610 --> 00:21:25,170
A really common thing here is actually

439
00:21:25,170 --> 00:21:29,090
that we look at this taking an infinity norm

440
00:21:29,090 --> 00:21:30,470
over the perturbation.

441
00:21:30,470 --> 00:21:33,250
So some intuition here is, instead of just looking

442
00:21:33,250 --> 00:21:37,950
at the aggregate difference between the image

443
00:21:37,950 --> 00:21:41,830
and the new one, instead, you're actually constraining it such

444
00:21:41,830 --> 00:21:44,590
that the largest change for any given pixel

445
00:21:44,590 --> 00:21:47,470
has to be less than a certain amount.

446
00:21:47,470 --> 00:21:49,030
So the infinity norm essentially is

447
00:21:49,030 --> 00:21:54,590
saying you're going to actually just keep the maximum change

448
00:21:54,590 --> 00:21:56,910
for any pixel small.

449
00:21:56,910 --> 00:21:59,710
And that does tend to actually result in things that

450
00:21:59,710 --> 00:22:03,830
are unperceptible to humans.

451
00:22:03,830 --> 00:22:05,110
Cool.

452
00:22:05,110 --> 00:22:09,110
So how do you actually do this?

453
00:22:09,110 --> 00:22:11,630
How do you, given some machine learning model

454
00:22:11,630 --> 00:22:13,590
that you assume is not changing, how do you

455
00:22:13,590 --> 00:22:16,150
find that maximum perturbation?

456
00:22:16,150 --> 00:22:19,210
Instead of trying to solve this as an optimization problem,

457
00:22:19,210 --> 00:22:23,128
you can actually also basically flip SGD on its head

458
00:22:23,128 --> 00:22:24,670
and you can do this thing that people

459
00:22:24,670 --> 00:22:28,950
call projected gradient ascent, where we're updating that data

460
00:22:28,950 --> 00:22:30,710
perturbation over time.

461
00:22:30,710 --> 00:22:32,840
And so first, you would take a step

462
00:22:32,840 --> 00:22:36,640
in the direction of the gradient,

463
00:22:36,640 --> 00:22:41,100
and then you project the result back into your feasible set.

464
00:22:41,100 --> 00:22:43,440
So you're constraining the amount

465
00:22:43,440 --> 00:22:45,540
that these images can change by design.

466
00:22:45,540 --> 00:22:49,000
You're making sure that it's obeying that optimization

467
00:22:49,000 --> 00:22:51,360
constraint that delta can't be too large.

468
00:22:51,360 --> 00:22:54,920
And then you just repeat this over and over again.

469
00:22:54,920 --> 00:22:58,368
It's quite simple and it actually works quite well.

470
00:22:58,368 --> 00:23:00,160
So how do you actually defend against this?

471
00:23:00,160 --> 00:23:03,360
Is there any way to defend against these active adversarial

472
00:23:03,360 --> 00:23:05,720
examples?

473
00:23:05,720 --> 00:23:09,860
Another way to think about this adversarial example,

474
00:23:09,860 --> 00:23:13,040
instead of trying to-- yeah?

475
00:23:13,040 --> 00:23:17,120
AUDIENCE: For the previous slides, how do you actually--

476
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:21,320
what kind of algorithms can you use to actually find the delta?

477
00:23:21,320 --> 00:23:24,080
Do you need, like, for example, access to the gradients

478
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:26,012
of the original model?

479
00:23:26,012 --> 00:23:26,720
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

480
00:23:26,720 --> 00:23:27,220
Yeah.

481
00:23:27,220 --> 00:23:29,320
So this projected gradient ascent

482
00:23:29,320 --> 00:23:32,070
assumes that you have the model weights and the architecture

483
00:23:32,070 --> 00:23:34,050
so you could actually calculate the gradients.

484
00:23:34,050 --> 00:23:34,550
Yeah.

485
00:23:34,550 --> 00:23:37,902
AUDIENCE: Is there a method that doesn't need the gradients?

486
00:23:37,902 --> 00:23:39,610
SARA BEERY: So the question is if there's

487
00:23:39,610 --> 00:23:42,670
methods that don't need the actual gradients of the model.

488
00:23:42,670 --> 00:23:43,170
Yeah.

489
00:23:43,170 --> 00:23:45,730
I mean, you could do it in a brute force way too, right?

490
00:23:45,730 --> 00:23:47,855
You could just think about trying a bunch of things

491
00:23:47,855 --> 00:23:49,410
and just picking the one greedily

492
00:23:49,410 --> 00:23:51,530
that the result was worst.

493
00:23:51,530 --> 00:23:53,870
Might be more expensive.

494
00:23:53,870 --> 00:23:57,930
So this is just maybe a more efficient way to find something.

495
00:23:57,930 --> 00:23:58,970
Yeah.

496
00:23:58,970 --> 00:24:01,370
And then there are other methods that

497
00:24:01,370 --> 00:24:04,630
try to do this, maybe in different ways.

498
00:24:04,630 --> 00:24:08,273
So not necessarily assuming that the model can't change.

499
00:24:08,273 --> 00:24:10,190
But that's, of course, a bit more complicated.

500
00:24:13,570 --> 00:24:16,490
A different way to think about finding an adversarial example

501
00:24:16,490 --> 00:24:19,570
is instead of trying to explicitly maximize

502
00:24:19,570 --> 00:24:23,730
the probability that your image is predicted as a given

503
00:24:23,730 --> 00:24:25,610
label, you can also think about it

504
00:24:25,610 --> 00:24:32,780
as just maximizing the loss between your model's prediction

505
00:24:32,780 --> 00:24:35,300
of the image plus that adversarial amount

506
00:24:35,300 --> 00:24:37,220
and the correct label, y.

507
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:39,520
Here, you're not saying, I really want this to look--

508
00:24:39,520 --> 00:24:41,800
I want the model to think it's this specific category.

509
00:24:41,800 --> 00:24:43,900
You're just saying, OK, what's the noise that

510
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:46,860
will make this most wrong, it'll make the loss

511
00:24:46,860 --> 00:24:48,780
highest for this example.

512
00:24:48,780 --> 00:24:50,500
Whereas, in standard training, you're

513
00:24:50,500 --> 00:24:53,740
trying to minimize that loss over all the possible thetas

514
00:24:53,740 --> 00:24:56,460
in the model itself.

515
00:24:56,460 --> 00:24:59,180
So then what you can think about doing

516
00:24:59,180 --> 00:25:01,780
is like, if you have standard training where you're using SGD

517
00:25:01,780 --> 00:25:05,020
and you're trying to minimize the loss over all

518
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:06,820
of the different data points in your model,

519
00:25:06,820 --> 00:25:09,860
you can think about training adversarially,

520
00:25:09,860 --> 00:25:11,300
trying to actually get your model

521
00:25:11,300 --> 00:25:14,820
to be robust to the types of perturbations

522
00:25:14,820 --> 00:25:17,380
that we're expecting this adversarial model

523
00:25:17,380 --> 00:25:19,480
might have-- this adversarial approach might have.

524
00:25:19,480 --> 00:25:24,320
So here, now, you have this two-loop,

525
00:25:24,320 --> 00:25:26,580
this nested optimization.

526
00:25:26,580 --> 00:25:31,320
So now, you're trying to find the model such

527
00:25:31,320 --> 00:25:36,360
that for every image in your data set,

528
00:25:36,360 --> 00:25:45,680
the worst case perturbation is still not too bad, essentially.

529
00:25:45,680 --> 00:25:48,560
You're trying to, during training,

530
00:25:48,560 --> 00:25:52,720
find the worst case perturbation and then find the model

531
00:25:52,720 --> 00:25:55,555
that for all of the different--

532
00:25:55,555 --> 00:25:57,680
all the different data points in your training data

533
00:25:57,680 --> 00:26:00,560
set, is still as good as possible,

534
00:26:00,560 --> 00:26:02,720
even in those worst cases.

535
00:26:02,720 --> 00:26:06,000
Does that make sense?

536
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:09,120
This also can be called adaptive data augmentation.

537
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:13,800
And it's an interesting example of finding these hardest cases.

538
00:26:13,800 --> 00:26:18,300
So we learned a while ago about hard example mining.

539
00:26:18,300 --> 00:26:20,760
It's maybe most informative, sometimes,

540
00:26:20,760 --> 00:26:23,680
if you show models explicit examples of things

541
00:26:23,680 --> 00:26:27,160
are going to be hard and you want it still do well.

542
00:26:27,160 --> 00:26:28,150
Yeah?

543
00:26:28,150 --> 00:26:31,210
AUDIENCE: What happens if there are two similarly hard

544
00:26:31,210 --> 00:26:35,310
examples, but very different from each other?

545
00:26:35,310 --> 00:26:36,810
SARA BEERY: So the question is, what

546
00:26:36,810 --> 00:26:39,250
happens if you have two hard examples that

547
00:26:39,250 --> 00:26:41,930
are different from each other.

548
00:26:41,930 --> 00:26:46,530
AUDIENCE: In this case, like, the maximally hard example

549
00:26:46,530 --> 00:26:51,130
for the turtle was rifle, but besides rifle, there's

550
00:26:51,130 --> 00:26:59,070
apple that is on a similar scale as being the rifle example,

551
00:26:59,070 --> 00:27:00,850
but basically, you've only trained

552
00:27:00,850 --> 00:27:05,610
for an adversarial example where it would be

553
00:27:05,610 --> 00:27:08,850
something related to a rifle.

554
00:27:08,850 --> 00:27:09,710
SARA BEERY: OK.

555
00:27:09,710 --> 00:27:11,910
I think I get what you're asking.

556
00:27:11,910 --> 00:27:12,630
No, no, no.

557
00:27:12,630 --> 00:27:15,090
I actually think it's a pretty prescient question,

558
00:27:15,090 --> 00:27:16,910
it's just worded a little complicatedly.

559
00:27:16,910 --> 00:27:19,850
So essentially the question is like, what

560
00:27:19,850 --> 00:27:23,250
if the data changes in a way you didn't anticipate.

561
00:27:23,250 --> 00:27:26,170
And so this is really explicitly constrained

562
00:27:26,170 --> 00:27:28,900
to be robust to the types of perturbations

563
00:27:28,900 --> 00:27:32,700
that you've defined as within your reasonable set

564
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:34,553
of perturbations, right?

565
00:27:34,553 --> 00:27:36,220
The cases we talked about were something

566
00:27:36,220 --> 00:27:39,980
that looked very small amounts of noise added to images.

567
00:27:39,980 --> 00:27:45,500
But we'll talk about in a bit, most perturbations of data

568
00:27:45,500 --> 00:27:48,120
are not of that structure in the real world.

569
00:27:48,120 --> 00:27:51,420
Often, if you have something that's

570
00:27:51,420 --> 00:27:55,600
out of distribution in some way, adversarial in some way,

571
00:27:55,600 --> 00:27:58,860
it's not specifically the static noise.

572
00:27:58,860 --> 00:28:00,460
And for example, this type of approach

573
00:28:00,460 --> 00:28:03,140
would probably not work very well for those stickers

574
00:28:03,140 --> 00:28:06,620
because that's a different type of perturbation of an image.

575
00:28:06,620 --> 00:28:07,837
Did you have a question?

576
00:28:07,837 --> 00:28:09,420
AUDIENCE: I was basically going to ask

577
00:28:09,420 --> 00:28:12,740
[INAUDIBLE] is that this won't help your self-driving car

578
00:28:12,740 --> 00:28:14,900
detect a pedestrian crosswalk.

579
00:28:14,900 --> 00:28:17,380
It's like, that's not a perturbation that would

580
00:28:17,380 --> 00:28:18,992
be modeled mathematically.

581
00:28:18,992 --> 00:28:19,700
SARA BEERY: Yeah.

582
00:28:19,700 --> 00:28:22,920
Or, I mean, pedestrian in a crosswalk, probably,

583
00:28:22,920 --> 00:28:25,710
you could reasonably say you could think about ways

584
00:28:25,710 --> 00:28:27,470
to augment your data to capture that

585
00:28:27,470 --> 00:28:29,720
in a bunch of different ways, but then what you end up

586
00:28:29,720 --> 00:28:30,990
with is weird stuff.

587
00:28:30,990 --> 00:28:33,310
Like, there's this classic example

588
00:28:33,310 --> 00:28:35,910
of a bunch of stop signs being transported

589
00:28:35,910 --> 00:28:38,130
by a truck in the back of a truck,

590
00:28:38,130 --> 00:28:40,750
and there's a self-driving car behind a truck full of stop

591
00:28:40,750 --> 00:28:42,390
signs, and it's just freaking out

592
00:28:42,390 --> 00:28:46,030
because what is it supposed to do?

593
00:28:46,030 --> 00:28:49,950
And so, yeah, this is, again, like I

594
00:28:49,950 --> 00:28:52,550
said before, it's just really hard to anticipate every way

595
00:28:52,550 --> 00:28:54,050
the world is going to change.

596
00:28:54,050 --> 00:28:56,230
So if you could do that, then you could reasonably

597
00:28:56,230 --> 00:29:00,230
try to be robust, either just through standard training,

598
00:29:00,230 --> 00:29:02,410
or if you didn't have the right training data,

599
00:29:02,410 --> 00:29:04,370
but you knew something about the way the world might change,

600
00:29:04,370 --> 00:29:05,990
you could try to be robust explicitly

601
00:29:05,990 --> 00:29:07,032
to those types of things.

602
00:29:07,032 --> 00:29:09,590
You could use things empirical risk minimization.

603
00:29:09,590 --> 00:29:13,690
But that is often not the case in reality.

604
00:29:13,690 --> 00:29:17,050
And so a lot of the work in adversarial example,

605
00:29:17,050 --> 00:29:19,530
adversarial robustness, they have to make assumptions,

606
00:29:19,530 --> 00:29:22,550
and usually, those assumptions are violated in real world.

607
00:29:22,550 --> 00:29:24,040
Unfortunately.

608
00:29:24,040 --> 00:29:24,573
Yes?

609
00:29:24,573 --> 00:29:26,240
AUDIENCE: Is there anything stopping you

610
00:29:26,240 --> 00:29:29,720
from a continuous loop of minimizing the loss

611
00:29:29,720 --> 00:29:32,800
and finding an adversarial example that maximizes the loss

612
00:29:32,800 --> 00:29:34,820
and just continue doing the back and forth?

613
00:29:34,820 --> 00:29:41,280
Or can a model eventually be trained so that you can't?

614
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:46,400
SARA BEERY: So for a reasonable set of perturbations,

615
00:29:46,400 --> 00:29:50,360
and assuming that maybe you try a bunch

616
00:29:50,360 --> 00:29:52,960
of different hyperparameters, it usually

617
00:29:52,960 --> 00:29:55,760
is often possible to get these to converge.

618
00:29:55,760 --> 00:29:57,960
But it can be difficult. Because you basically

619
00:29:57,960 --> 00:30:01,200
have two different optimization objectives that are conflicting.

620
00:30:01,200 --> 00:30:04,960
And so they can be pretty finicky to train.

621
00:30:04,960 --> 00:30:06,840
Yeah.

622
00:30:06,840 --> 00:30:07,920
Cool.

623
00:30:07,920 --> 00:30:09,670
So now, if you're actually doing that type

624
00:30:09,670 --> 00:30:12,560
of adversarial training, what you actually do in the training

625
00:30:12,560 --> 00:30:14,520
loop is you would sample some data point

626
00:30:14,520 --> 00:30:16,200
x with the ground truth y.

627
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:20,280
You would compute now the approximate optimal adversarial

628
00:30:20,280 --> 00:30:21,140
perturbation.

629
00:30:21,140 --> 00:30:24,635
And you could do that with that projected gradient ascent.

630
00:30:24,635 --> 00:30:26,260
And then you would compute the gradient

631
00:30:26,260 --> 00:30:27,820
given that perturbation.

632
00:30:27,820 --> 00:30:30,180
And then you would update your loss

633
00:30:30,180 --> 00:30:31,920
given that gradient for the parameters.

634
00:30:31,920 --> 00:30:36,660
So essentially, for every model, you're

635
00:30:36,660 --> 00:30:39,780
trained-- for every data point, you're training another model.

636
00:30:39,780 --> 00:30:41,640
And we'll talk a bit about it next lecture,

637
00:30:41,640 --> 00:30:45,080
but this also is similar to things like meta learning,

638
00:30:45,080 --> 00:30:48,277
where, potentially, in your outer model training loop

639
00:30:48,277 --> 00:30:49,860
now for every data point, you actually

640
00:30:49,860 --> 00:30:51,660
have an inner training loop.

641
00:30:51,660 --> 00:30:53,580
So it's also quite computationally expensive

642
00:30:53,580 --> 00:30:55,640
to do this.

643
00:30:55,640 --> 00:30:56,140
Cool.

644
00:30:56,140 --> 00:30:59,100
So what do these examples tell us?

645
00:30:59,100 --> 00:31:02,120
What does the fact that these adversarial examples exist

646
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:06,700
tell us about the models itself?

647
00:31:06,700 --> 00:31:09,260
Arguably, I would say it tells us

648
00:31:09,260 --> 00:31:12,840
something about the input features, the structure,

649
00:31:12,840 --> 00:31:15,260
the statistical characteristics of the data

650
00:31:15,260 --> 00:31:18,140
that we're training the model on that become critical

651
00:31:18,140 --> 00:31:20,500
for the model's decision.

652
00:31:20,500 --> 00:31:22,430
There's an interesting example here.

653
00:31:22,430 --> 00:31:25,870
This is training data trying to teach a model to categorize

654
00:31:25,870 --> 00:31:27,990
4's versus 9's.

655
00:31:27,990 --> 00:31:30,930
And the interesting-- so this is MNIST, essentially.

656
00:31:30,930 --> 00:31:34,110
But the interesting point here is that what they've done

657
00:31:34,110 --> 00:31:37,110
is they've added spurious correlations that

658
00:31:37,110 --> 00:31:38,350
are very small.

659
00:31:38,350 --> 00:31:42,670
So for every 4, they've added a white pixel

660
00:31:42,670 --> 00:31:45,110
in the top left corner, and for every 9,

661
00:31:45,110 --> 00:31:48,270
they've added a white pixel just one next to that.

662
00:31:48,270 --> 00:31:50,713
So now, what ends up happening is

663
00:31:50,713 --> 00:31:52,630
if you do these adversarial perturbations that

664
00:31:52,630 --> 00:31:54,190
are very targeted, you basically just

665
00:31:54,190 --> 00:31:56,350
change the location of that pixel,

666
00:31:56,350 --> 00:31:59,390
you get the exact opposite prediction.

667
00:31:59,390 --> 00:32:01,830
And this really gets at this question of,

668
00:32:01,830 --> 00:32:04,830
what are these models learning?

669
00:32:04,830 --> 00:32:07,590
And they're going to learn whatever signals

670
00:32:07,590 --> 00:32:12,190
are there, and ideally, whatever signals are easiest to pick up.

671
00:32:12,190 --> 00:32:15,110
So this becomes just a binary classifier

672
00:32:15,110 --> 00:32:17,595
if you consistently have these correlations.

673
00:32:17,595 --> 00:32:19,470
The model just ignores the rest of the image.

674
00:32:19,470 --> 00:32:22,140
It learns all it has to look at is just that top pixel

675
00:32:22,140 --> 00:32:25,000
and it's a perfect categorizer.

676
00:32:25,000 --> 00:32:28,840
So what this can tell us is that for any given

677
00:32:28,840 --> 00:32:33,600
model, the features that are captured in that model,

678
00:32:33,600 --> 00:32:36,920
some amount of them will be useless, some amount of them

679
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:38,280
will be robust.

680
00:32:38,280 --> 00:32:41,540
So these are features that are correlated with the label,

681
00:32:41,540 --> 00:32:44,560
even if they've been perturbed in some way.

682
00:32:44,560 --> 00:32:47,740
So here, they're showing things that are quite semantic,

683
00:32:47,740 --> 00:32:52,140
things like specific attributes of a cat ear, a dog nose,

684
00:32:52,140 --> 00:32:56,053
or the structural shape of an animal.

685
00:32:56,053 --> 00:32:57,720
And then there's another set of features

686
00:32:57,720 --> 00:32:59,420
that you could argue are not robust.

687
00:32:59,420 --> 00:33:00,840
And these are going to be features

688
00:33:00,840 --> 00:33:04,080
that are correlated with the label in the training data set,

689
00:33:04,080 --> 00:33:06,780
but can be flipped with perturbation.

690
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:10,280
So perturbing these non-robust features

691
00:33:10,280 --> 00:33:13,120
can actually change your prediction.

692
00:33:13,120 --> 00:33:14,920
And so many features can be correlated

693
00:33:14,920 --> 00:33:17,040
with the label in your training data set,

694
00:33:17,040 --> 00:33:18,460
and thus, be predictive.

695
00:33:18,460 --> 00:33:19,930
And they might also be correlated

696
00:33:19,930 --> 00:33:23,170
with the label in your test data set and be predictive.

697
00:33:23,170 --> 00:33:25,010
And so they can help with accuracy.

698
00:33:25,010 --> 00:33:27,690
Like, your accuracy is good, but they're

699
00:33:27,690 --> 00:33:31,050
features that are beyond what a human would use, or sometimes,

700
00:33:31,050 --> 00:33:35,890
maybe, in some subtle cases, things that we might use based

701
00:33:35,890 --> 00:33:39,830
on stereotypes, but if we think rationally about it,

702
00:33:39,830 --> 00:33:43,050
we might not want to use them.

703
00:33:43,050 --> 00:33:46,930
There's a lot of examples of these types of stereotypes

704
00:33:46,930 --> 00:33:51,410
or biases in training data sets that are against women or gender

705
00:33:51,410 --> 00:33:52,170
minorities.

706
00:33:52,170 --> 00:33:54,450
Like if you show--

707
00:33:54,450 --> 00:33:56,610
early ChatGPT was very, very popular.

708
00:33:56,610 --> 00:34:00,650
Oh, if you asked it to generate-- or not ChatGPT.

709
00:34:00,650 --> 00:34:01,390
Diffusion models.

710
00:34:01,390 --> 00:34:03,390
If you asked it to generate images of a doctor,

711
00:34:03,390 --> 00:34:04,765
it gave you a bunch of white men.

712
00:34:04,765 --> 00:34:09,710
You asked it to generate images of a homemaker,

713
00:34:09,710 --> 00:34:11,889
it would give you a bunch of women.

714
00:34:11,889 --> 00:34:15,370
Like, biases that are built into the data sets, that

715
00:34:15,370 --> 00:34:20,420
might be correlated with society, but unfortunately,

716
00:34:20,420 --> 00:34:22,580
are things that, if we think about it

717
00:34:22,580 --> 00:34:24,980
from an ethical perspective, we don't want

718
00:34:24,980 --> 00:34:27,460
models to make decisions on.

719
00:34:27,460 --> 00:34:29,159
This gets really complicated.

720
00:34:29,159 --> 00:34:31,699
So there's this entire deep literature

721
00:34:31,699 --> 00:34:34,100
on fairness in machine learning models

722
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:36,580
and how you think about equity when

723
00:34:36,580 --> 00:34:38,580
the data we're training models on

724
00:34:38,580 --> 00:34:42,020
is just as biased as our modern society.

725
00:34:42,020 --> 00:34:45,940
And these models tend to exacerbate societal biases.

726
00:34:45,940 --> 00:34:48,940
So correlative predictive features

727
00:34:48,940 --> 00:34:52,594
are not always something we want the model to use,

728
00:34:52,594 --> 00:34:54,219
both because it can make it non-robust,

729
00:34:54,219 --> 00:34:56,653
but also because it can exacerbate bias.

730
00:34:56,653 --> 00:34:58,320
And where do the correlations come from?

731
00:34:58,320 --> 00:34:59,612
They really come from the data.

732
00:35:02,140 --> 00:35:05,820
This is a fun example, dogs and cats.

733
00:35:05,820 --> 00:35:07,618
Turns out, if you put a dog in a bow tie,

734
00:35:07,618 --> 00:35:09,660
the model will predict it's a cat because there's

735
00:35:09,660 --> 00:35:13,700
this weird bias where people put bow ties on cats more than dogs.

736
00:35:13,700 --> 00:35:15,540
Or this is a really fun one.

737
00:35:15,540 --> 00:35:18,120
If you look at all the images of fish

738
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:19,820
in the ImageNet training data set,

739
00:35:19,820 --> 00:35:23,800
you get grumpy dudes in sunglasses.

740
00:35:23,800 --> 00:35:25,420
Maybe very happy dudes in sunglasses.

741
00:35:25,420 --> 00:35:26,160
They're going fishing.

742
00:35:26,160 --> 00:35:26,702
They love it.

743
00:35:26,702 --> 00:35:28,900
But the model's not learned fish.

744
00:35:28,900 --> 00:35:33,000
It's learned like fish in the hands of a person.

745
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:37,680
And then, basically, this really is just

746
00:35:37,680 --> 00:35:40,440
rooted in how we create data sets.

747
00:35:40,440 --> 00:35:43,080
So in an ideal world, we would have

748
00:35:43,080 --> 00:35:45,640
representative real-world images that

749
00:35:45,640 --> 00:35:47,960
captured the distribution of everything we would ever

750
00:35:47,960 --> 00:35:49,000
care about.

751
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:51,400
We would have expert annotators who were then

752
00:35:51,400 --> 00:35:54,840
able to build perfect annotations on top of that,

753
00:35:54,840 --> 00:35:57,180
and then we could use that to carefully and meaningfully

754
00:35:57,180 --> 00:35:58,680
construct a benchmark where we could

755
00:35:58,680 --> 00:36:02,120
evaluate machine learning performance and happy sun

756
00:36:02,120 --> 00:36:03,280
and rainbows.

757
00:36:03,280 --> 00:36:05,240
But in the real world, we often have

758
00:36:05,240 --> 00:36:08,160
images scraped from the internet,

759
00:36:08,160 --> 00:36:10,840
and then we have some form of automated or crowd-collected

760
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:14,180
labels, and then that results in these noisy,

761
00:36:14,180 --> 00:36:16,570
complicated, biased annotations, and that

762
00:36:16,570 --> 00:36:19,670
might create an easy to optimize benchmark,

763
00:36:19,670 --> 00:36:21,810
but it's not necessarily something

764
00:36:21,810 --> 00:36:24,250
that is rooted in reality.

765
00:36:24,250 --> 00:36:27,690
And one argument is that all these models

766
00:36:27,690 --> 00:36:29,190
are learning are shortcuts.

767
00:36:29,190 --> 00:36:31,393
It's all different types of pattern matching.

768
00:36:31,393 --> 00:36:32,810
And so shortcuts here are features

769
00:36:32,810 --> 00:36:35,265
that are correlated with the label in the training data,

770
00:36:35,265 --> 00:36:36,890
but maybe not correlated with the label

771
00:36:36,890 --> 00:36:38,810
under realistic distribution shifts.

772
00:36:38,810 --> 00:36:41,810
And it turns out that these models will use them

773
00:36:41,810 --> 00:36:44,450
and will not generalize if then those features are

774
00:36:44,450 --> 00:36:45,710
no longer correlated.

775
00:36:48,290 --> 00:36:53,050
Interestingly, there's a dimension of imbalance here.

776
00:36:53,050 --> 00:36:55,390
In some of the things that I try to do,

777
00:36:55,390 --> 00:36:59,090
I'm doing species identification in static cameras,

778
00:36:59,090 --> 00:37:05,850
and what we get is day-night bias for nondiurnal species.

779
00:37:05,850 --> 00:37:11,370
Raccoons show up a lot at night, and we still

780
00:37:11,370 --> 00:37:14,460
have some images of raccoons during the day during training,

781
00:37:14,460 --> 00:37:16,620
but the vast majority are at night.

782
00:37:16,620 --> 00:37:19,740
So the model's learned that it's like, nighttime, kind

783
00:37:19,740 --> 00:37:22,300
of like, fat, cat blob thing.

784
00:37:22,300 --> 00:37:23,940
Probably a raccoon.

785
00:37:23,940 --> 00:37:26,688
And if you show it, daytime raccoon images,

786
00:37:26,688 --> 00:37:28,480
even though it's seen them during training,

787
00:37:28,480 --> 00:37:30,220
the performance is just much worse.

788
00:37:30,220 --> 00:37:35,740
Because this correlative predictive factor is not there.

789
00:37:35,740 --> 00:37:38,053
So the models will use these shortcuts

790
00:37:38,053 --> 00:37:40,720
when they're provided because we train them based on likelihood.

791
00:37:40,720 --> 00:37:44,300
And this is almost-- this is often

792
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:45,720
related to the data itself.

793
00:37:45,720 --> 00:37:48,880
And so, interestingly, adversarial examples,

794
00:37:48,880 --> 00:37:52,820
those types of perturbations we talked about

795
00:37:52,820 --> 00:37:56,220
can transfer across models trained on the same data set.

796
00:37:56,220 --> 00:37:59,820
So the same types of shortcuts will often

797
00:37:59,820 --> 00:38:03,820
be learned, even if you use different architectures,

798
00:38:03,820 --> 00:38:06,660
because it's trained on the same data set.

799
00:38:06,660 --> 00:38:09,980
And what can these shortcuts look like?

800
00:38:09,980 --> 00:38:14,670
Yeah, things like this is a picture of a green hillside,

801
00:38:14,670 --> 00:38:18,750
and this model is explicitly saying a herd of sheep grazing

802
00:38:18,750 --> 00:38:22,470
on a hillside, but there's no sheep.

803
00:38:22,470 --> 00:38:25,670
This one says a flock of birds flying in the air.

804
00:38:25,670 --> 00:38:29,062
These are instead these really nice tree-based goats.

805
00:38:29,062 --> 00:38:30,770
I don't know if you guys have seen these.

806
00:38:30,770 --> 00:38:31,450
They're amazing.

807
00:38:31,450 --> 00:38:34,590
They're goats that climb trees in the Mediterranean.

808
00:38:34,590 --> 00:38:36,950
And then, things like this.

809
00:38:36,950 --> 00:38:40,110
It's people holding goats, but the model

810
00:38:40,110 --> 00:38:41,590
predicts they're dogs because it's

811
00:38:41,590 --> 00:38:44,830
seen less of these examples of people holding dogs.

812
00:38:44,830 --> 00:38:46,310
And these can be--

813
00:38:46,310 --> 00:38:48,950
these are all silly, but they can be pretty problematic

814
00:38:48,950 --> 00:38:49,530
as well.

815
00:38:52,510 --> 00:38:54,750
There was some early work that was showing

816
00:38:54,750 --> 00:38:58,230
that you could do a lot of really

817
00:38:58,230 --> 00:39:01,490
nice predictive diagnostics on X-rays,

818
00:39:01,490 --> 00:39:04,470
and then there was this pushback work that showed that, actually,

819
00:39:04,470 --> 00:39:07,830
what the models were learning was to detect where

820
00:39:07,830 --> 00:39:11,520
the X-ray was acquired, so which hospital, and then

821
00:39:11,520 --> 00:39:13,120
calibrate the predictions according

822
00:39:13,120 --> 00:39:15,880
to the distribution of diseases at that hospital, which

823
00:39:15,880 --> 00:39:19,640
is, again, probably the type of useful,

824
00:39:19,640 --> 00:39:22,920
but unwanted correlation.

825
00:39:22,920 --> 00:39:26,540
Or similarly, it's learning that if an image has a ruler in it,

826
00:39:26,540 --> 00:39:30,080
an algorithm is more likely to call a tumor malignant.

827
00:39:30,080 --> 00:39:32,280
And so you can imagine now that, essentially,

828
00:39:32,280 --> 00:39:33,760
what this is meaning is like you're

829
00:39:33,760 --> 00:39:37,130
much less likely to detect the examples where a doctor hasn't

830
00:39:37,130 --> 00:39:38,880
already thought that it might be malignant

831
00:39:38,880 --> 00:39:41,320
and is measuring it with a ruler.

832
00:39:41,320 --> 00:39:43,580
So not all predictive patterns are desirable.

833
00:39:43,580 --> 00:39:46,040
And we talked about those fairness examples as well.

834
00:39:46,040 --> 00:39:48,525
And there are tons of examples of this.

835
00:39:48,525 --> 00:39:50,400
You can think about this in interesting ways.

836
00:39:50,400 --> 00:40:00,298
So here, this is essentially going from IID to OOD--

837
00:40:00,298 --> 00:40:01,840
independently identically distributed

838
00:40:01,840 --> 00:40:04,400
to out-of-distribution.

839
00:40:04,400 --> 00:40:08,480
You can see domain shift, adversarial examples,

840
00:40:08,480 --> 00:40:14,400
types of distortions, pose warping, texture change,

841
00:40:14,400 --> 00:40:15,520
background shifts.

842
00:40:15,520 --> 00:40:18,700
All of these will often result in a human looking

843
00:40:18,700 --> 00:40:21,280
at these images and saying it is the same category,

844
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:25,610
but the model ends up not predicting the same category.

845
00:40:25,610 --> 00:40:27,860
So this is things that we want the model to generalize

846
00:40:27,860 --> 00:40:29,380
on, but it doesn't.

847
00:40:29,380 --> 00:40:31,000
And then there's the opposite too,

848
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:33,740
which is things where the machine learning model is

849
00:40:33,740 --> 00:40:35,920
very convinced it's the same category,

850
00:40:35,920 --> 00:40:38,300
but a human would not be.

851
00:40:38,300 --> 00:40:42,300
And this happens for transformer models as well.

852
00:40:42,300 --> 00:40:47,300
This was a case where people had trained transformer models

853
00:40:47,300 --> 00:40:52,860
to look at reasoning, but it turned out

854
00:40:52,860 --> 00:40:55,580
that they pretty explicitly couldn't generalize out

855
00:40:55,580 --> 00:40:58,140
of the distribution of the number of the sequence lengths

856
00:40:58,140 --> 00:40:59,280
that they were trained on.

857
00:41:02,220 --> 00:41:04,980
So adversarial training, when you're

858
00:41:04,980 --> 00:41:07,220
training it to be robust to these types

859
00:41:07,220 --> 00:41:10,110
of non-robust features, and now, here, we're

860
00:41:10,110 --> 00:41:13,090
assuming that we know the dimensions of robustness,

861
00:41:13,090 --> 00:41:16,590
which, of course, we don't often know,

862
00:41:16,590 --> 00:41:19,550
they do tend to teach models that

863
00:41:19,550 --> 00:41:22,230
have outputs that are hopefully stable under these types

864
00:41:22,230 --> 00:41:24,450
of adversarial perturbations.

865
00:41:24,450 --> 00:41:28,590
So you get invariance to these non-robust features.

866
00:41:28,590 --> 00:41:33,790
And of course, this type of just noise addition

867
00:41:33,790 --> 00:41:35,230
is not necessarily representative

868
00:41:35,230 --> 00:41:39,030
of every possible way that this data could be perturbed,

869
00:41:39,030 --> 00:41:41,510
but there are some nice papers that

870
00:41:41,510 --> 00:41:44,110
show that if you train in this adversarial way,

871
00:41:44,110 --> 00:41:46,910
it can be much more expensive, but you

872
00:41:46,910 --> 00:41:54,310
can get features that tend to be more human intuitive.

873
00:41:54,310 --> 00:41:57,910
I'm always a little bit iffy on the use of things

874
00:41:57,910 --> 00:42:02,540
like these activation heat maps as concrete explainers

875
00:42:02,540 --> 00:42:04,290
of what a machine learning model is doing,

876
00:42:04,290 --> 00:42:05,870
but these do look interesting.

877
00:42:05,870 --> 00:42:12,840
So here, if you look at a primate, here's just some--

878
00:42:12,840 --> 00:42:15,540
if you train with an L infinity or an L2 norm,

879
00:42:15,540 --> 00:42:18,080
you can see that the features are a little bit more

880
00:42:18,080 --> 00:42:21,160
semantically meaningful, they look a little bit more

881
00:42:21,160 --> 00:42:24,440
like the object of interest.

882
00:42:24,440 --> 00:42:29,840
And similarly, if you're trying to build these adversarial

883
00:42:29,840 --> 00:42:31,700
examples for robust models-- now,

884
00:42:31,700 --> 00:42:34,520
if you've trained it to be adversarial adversarially

885
00:42:34,520 --> 00:42:37,240
robust, then the thing that you're--

886
00:42:37,240 --> 00:42:39,200
the data that your model actually

887
00:42:39,200 --> 00:42:44,920
generates to be adversarial has to look a little bit more

888
00:42:44,920 --> 00:42:46,560
like the semantic category you're

889
00:42:46,560 --> 00:42:48,680
trying to make adversarial to.

890
00:42:48,680 --> 00:42:50,640
So here, if you start from these pigeons

891
00:42:50,640 --> 00:42:52,960
and you try to find the adversarial example that

892
00:42:52,960 --> 00:42:55,560
makes them look maximally like dogs, this kind of looks

893
00:42:55,560 --> 00:42:56,207
like dogs.

894
00:42:56,207 --> 00:42:58,540
It just really doesn't look like pigeons anymore at all.

895
00:42:58,540 --> 00:43:00,360
And so these are things that a human

896
00:43:00,360 --> 00:43:03,800
is more OK with these types of mistakes.

897
00:43:03,800 --> 00:43:07,490
And there are some papers that show that adversarial trend

898
00:43:07,490 --> 00:43:10,970
models maybe build representations

899
00:43:10,970 --> 00:43:12,930
that transfer better to other data

900
00:43:12,930 --> 00:43:18,650
sets, at least specifically with some of the data

901
00:43:18,650 --> 00:43:20,730
sets shown here.

902
00:43:20,730 --> 00:43:21,250
Cool.

903
00:43:21,250 --> 00:43:23,530
So adversarial training you can think

904
00:43:23,530 --> 00:43:26,930
of as like another very targeted form of maybe regularization

905
00:43:26,930 --> 00:43:29,030
or a different type of domain adaptation,

906
00:43:29,030 --> 00:43:30,530
but one that's quite targeted to try

907
00:43:30,530 --> 00:43:34,650
to make the models robust to some explicitly constructed form

908
00:43:34,650 --> 00:43:36,363
of perturbation.

909
00:43:36,363 --> 00:43:38,530
So now, what if we're not talking about perturbation

910
00:43:38,530 --> 00:43:40,410
on individual data points, but we're instead

911
00:43:40,410 --> 00:43:43,650
talking about perturbations over entire distributions?

912
00:43:43,650 --> 00:43:46,690
So here, instead of having the same distribution for training

913
00:43:46,690 --> 00:43:49,450
and inference, now, we have a pretty different distribution

914
00:43:49,450 --> 00:43:52,330
for training and inference.

915
00:43:52,330 --> 00:43:56,210
So this is basically the world I live in.

916
00:43:56,210 --> 00:43:59,690
Here, this on the left, that's a map

917
00:43:59,690 --> 00:44:02,210
of an estimate of alpha diversity

918
00:44:02,210 --> 00:44:05,570
globally, which is an estimate of, essentially,

919
00:44:05,570 --> 00:44:10,990
the number of unique species that exist in any given place.

920
00:44:10,990 --> 00:44:15,910
And then, on the right, this is a heat map of the three billion

921
00:44:15,910 --> 00:44:17,750
existing species occurrence records

922
00:44:17,750 --> 00:44:20,590
in the Global Biodiversity Information Facility.

923
00:44:20,590 --> 00:44:23,970
And you can see that they are clearly not super well-aligned.

924
00:44:23,970 --> 00:44:26,270
They're almost anti-correlated.

925
00:44:26,270 --> 00:44:30,430
So you can imagine that if this is my training distribution,

926
00:44:30,430 --> 00:44:33,370
and somehow, that's the distribution of the data

927
00:44:33,370 --> 00:44:36,070
maybe I want to use the model on that, that's a pretty

928
00:44:36,070 --> 00:44:38,950
significant distribution shift.

929
00:44:38,950 --> 00:44:41,670
And then, add on top of that the fact that all of it's

930
00:44:41,670 --> 00:44:45,527
changing all the time and it gets pretty messy.

931
00:44:45,527 --> 00:44:47,110
And this was the motivation, actually,

932
00:44:47,110 --> 00:44:49,570
behind the creation of this Wilds benchmark,

933
00:44:49,570 --> 00:44:53,443
which I built with many other very talented people.

934
00:44:53,443 --> 00:44:55,110
Specifically, it was reflecting the fact

935
00:44:55,110 --> 00:44:57,590
that a lot of the literature around distribution

936
00:44:57,590 --> 00:44:59,270
shift in the machine learning community

937
00:44:59,270 --> 00:45:03,950
were these types of not realistic distribution shifts,

938
00:45:03,950 --> 00:45:09,280
like, adding noise to things, or taking MNIST and rotating it

939
00:45:09,280 --> 00:45:13,880
by 45 degrees, or making it a different color.

940
00:45:13,880 --> 00:45:16,302
Not necessarily super representative.

941
00:45:16,302 --> 00:45:18,760
Even some of the examples that were designed on real data--

942
00:45:18,760 --> 00:45:20,700
I don't know if you've ever heard of the waterbirds data

943
00:45:20,700 --> 00:45:21,140
set.

944
00:45:21,140 --> 00:45:22,765
It's basically a data set of waterbirds

945
00:45:22,765 --> 00:45:27,600
where the training data has waterbirds not in the water,

946
00:45:27,600 --> 00:45:30,160
and the test data has them in the water.

947
00:45:30,160 --> 00:45:32,220
It's a background shift problem.

948
00:45:32,220 --> 00:45:33,560
But it's pretty artificial.

949
00:45:33,560 --> 00:45:36,820
You've constrained it so that the categories are balanced,

950
00:45:36,820 --> 00:45:39,760
that you're only looking at one dimension of shift,

951
00:45:39,760 --> 00:45:41,785
and the real world is quite messy.

952
00:45:41,785 --> 00:45:43,160
So this is a benchmark that looks

953
00:45:43,160 --> 00:45:46,240
at distribution shifts over a pretty diverse set

954
00:45:46,240 --> 00:45:48,360
of real-world domains, everything

955
00:45:48,360 --> 00:45:54,480
from different scans from hospitals,

956
00:45:54,480 --> 00:45:59,280
to different locations of species categorization,

957
00:45:59,280 --> 00:46:03,490
generalizing mapping of poverty across countries,

958
00:46:03,490 --> 00:46:08,090
looking at comments across demographics.

959
00:46:08,090 --> 00:46:11,930
And essentially, what this benchmark highlighted

960
00:46:11,930 --> 00:46:15,450
was that when you move out of distribution

961
00:46:15,450 --> 00:46:18,210
in these realistic ways, basically,

962
00:46:18,210 --> 00:46:21,370
model performance degrades significantly.

963
00:46:21,370 --> 00:46:24,530
And the way you actually think about measuring this is you

964
00:46:24,530 --> 00:46:26,370
can try to explicitly construct benchmarks

965
00:46:26,370 --> 00:46:30,070
that look at performance over realistic distribution shifts.

966
00:46:30,070 --> 00:46:34,130
So here, we looked at the gap between a control,

967
00:46:34,130 --> 00:46:36,610
in distribution test data-- and note that this is not

968
00:46:36,610 --> 00:46:38,270
actually identical.

969
00:46:38,270 --> 00:46:41,370
It's just randomly sampled from within the training data

970
00:46:41,370 --> 00:46:42,410
locations.

971
00:46:42,410 --> 00:46:44,290
And then you have some new cameras

972
00:46:44,290 --> 00:46:46,790
that are held out that are out of distribution.

973
00:46:46,790 --> 00:46:49,250
And you can see that macro F1 performance degrades

974
00:46:49,250 --> 00:46:52,010
by like, 16%, which is pretty significant.

975
00:46:52,010 --> 00:46:53,770
And if you're thinking about what

976
00:46:53,770 --> 00:46:55,450
are the factors that could lead to this,

977
00:46:55,450 --> 00:46:56,950
there's a bunch of different things.

978
00:46:56,950 --> 00:46:58,950
It could explicitly be things like background.

979
00:46:58,950 --> 00:47:00,830
It can be things like pose of cameras.

980
00:47:00,830 --> 00:47:04,322
So the top and the bottom are more horizontal.

981
00:47:04,322 --> 00:47:06,280
The one in the middle is posed a little higher,

982
00:47:06,280 --> 00:47:09,220
so you're getting different angles on species.

983
00:47:09,220 --> 00:47:11,880
Here, you can see some of them have passive infrared at night.

984
00:47:11,880 --> 00:47:13,080
Some of them have color.

985
00:47:13,080 --> 00:47:16,020
So the dimensions of shift in the real world

986
00:47:16,020 --> 00:47:18,260
can often be quite complex.

987
00:47:18,260 --> 00:47:19,880
But not just that visual shift.

988
00:47:19,880 --> 00:47:22,300
We also have pretty explicit what can be termed

989
00:47:22,300 --> 00:47:23,520
subpopulation shift.

990
00:47:23,520 --> 00:47:26,780
So essentially, for any given location or any given camera,

991
00:47:26,780 --> 00:47:29,260
it has its own unique distribution over the categories

992
00:47:29,260 --> 00:47:30,420
of interest.

993
00:47:30,420 --> 00:47:31,500
It makes sense.

994
00:47:31,500 --> 00:47:33,380
That camera is placed in a microhabitat.

995
00:47:33,380 --> 00:47:35,380
That microhabitat has a local set

996
00:47:35,380 --> 00:47:39,020
of species that's interacting with it.

997
00:47:39,020 --> 00:47:40,700
And it turns out that this is basically

998
00:47:40,700 --> 00:47:43,520
true for every ecological problem I've ever worked on.

999
00:47:43,520 --> 00:47:45,060
So different ecosystems will have

1000
00:47:45,060 --> 00:47:47,220
both subpopulation and visual distribution

1001
00:47:47,220 --> 00:47:48,820
shifts no matter what.

1002
00:47:48,820 --> 00:47:51,780
This is looking at detecting individual trees

1003
00:47:51,780 --> 00:47:54,540
from aerial data across the National Ecological Observatory

1004
00:47:54,540 --> 00:47:55,280
Network.

1005
00:47:55,280 --> 00:47:58,740
Trees both look different and are at different densities.

1006
00:47:58,740 --> 00:48:04,190
You can similarly try to think about categorizing detecting

1007
00:48:04,190 --> 00:48:06,430
and counting fish in different rivers

1008
00:48:06,430 --> 00:48:10,190
across the Pacific Northwest, or things

1009
00:48:10,190 --> 00:48:17,990
like categorizing urban trees across different cities in North

1010
00:48:17,990 --> 00:48:18,990
America.

1011
00:48:18,990 --> 00:48:20,910
And you can see that both visually

1012
00:48:20,910 --> 00:48:22,830
and from the sense of subpopulation here,

1013
00:48:22,830 --> 00:48:26,757
the colors are mapping the 20 most common genera of trees.

1014
00:48:26,757 --> 00:48:28,590
And the differences in colors between cities

1015
00:48:28,590 --> 00:48:31,487
capture that subpopulation distribution.

1016
00:48:31,487 --> 00:48:33,070
And it turns out the performance often

1017
00:48:33,070 --> 00:48:36,310
has a very strong correlation with subpopulation distribution

1018
00:48:36,310 --> 00:48:37,150
similarity.

1019
00:48:37,150 --> 00:48:40,510
So this is if you're looking at training on one city for urban

1020
00:48:40,510 --> 00:48:43,870
forests and testing on another, you actually get these kind

1021
00:48:43,870 --> 00:48:47,950
of block-wise structures where cities that have similar

1022
00:48:47,950 --> 00:48:50,850
training distributions will--

1023
00:48:50,850 --> 00:48:52,910
you'll generalize better within the set

1024
00:48:52,910 --> 00:48:55,210
of cities that have similar training distributions.

1025
00:48:55,210 --> 00:48:56,790
It's kind of intuitive.

1026
00:48:56,790 --> 00:48:58,850
These models are trained on likelihoods,

1027
00:48:58,850 --> 00:49:00,570
so you're almost building a prior

1028
00:49:00,570 --> 00:49:03,370
based on the subpopulation distribution within any given

1029
00:49:03,370 --> 00:49:04,410
training set.

1030
00:49:04,410 --> 00:49:06,710
And if you're matching that more closely,

1031
00:49:06,710 --> 00:49:10,450
your model is more likely to predict well.

1032
00:49:10,450 --> 00:49:13,090
And there's some nice examples of simple approaches

1033
00:49:13,090 --> 00:49:17,530
you can try to do to actually match the expected

1034
00:49:17,530 --> 00:49:20,330
distribution of your test data, even

1035
00:49:20,330 --> 00:49:21,630
without additional training.

1036
00:49:21,630 --> 00:49:25,835
So things like if you can build or if you can represent a prior,

1037
00:49:25,835 --> 00:49:27,210
if you have some way to construct

1038
00:49:27,210 --> 00:49:30,810
a prior over the set of categories you expect to see,

1039
00:49:30,810 --> 00:49:33,770
then you can just do a really simple Bayesian remapping

1040
00:49:33,770 --> 00:49:38,710
of the predicted probabilities to take into account that prior.

1041
00:49:38,710 --> 00:49:40,450
I'll talk about that a little.

1042
00:49:40,450 --> 00:49:42,930
So what do you do about distribution shift?

1043
00:49:42,930 --> 00:49:44,650
So one thing that people have looked at

1044
00:49:44,650 --> 00:49:48,090
is this that's quite similar to the adversarial example we

1045
00:49:48,090 --> 00:49:50,530
talked about before is something called distributionally

1046
00:49:50,530 --> 00:49:52,810
robust optimization.

1047
00:49:52,810 --> 00:49:56,250
So far, we were saying we were allowed to perturb each data

1048
00:49:56,250 --> 00:49:59,662
point by some limited amount, but an alternative

1049
00:49:59,662 --> 00:50:01,620
is that you can actually think about perturbing

1050
00:50:01,620 --> 00:50:06,540
the entire training distribution by some amount together.

1051
00:50:06,540 --> 00:50:10,740
And so now, what this looks like is distribution--

1052
00:50:10,740 --> 00:50:13,620
robust optimization essentially just assumes

1053
00:50:13,620 --> 00:50:17,620
that the worst case data distribution shift might occur.

1054
00:50:17,620 --> 00:50:20,580
So you might have some different samples

1055
00:50:20,580 --> 00:50:21,760
in terms of the densities.

1056
00:50:21,760 --> 00:50:25,420
You might take some of the data away, et cetera, et cetera.

1057
00:50:25,420 --> 00:50:28,500
And so they think about ways you can perturb the distribution

1058
00:50:28,500 --> 00:50:29,980
of your training data set.

1059
00:50:29,980 --> 00:50:32,260
And then they take a similar approach

1060
00:50:32,260 --> 00:50:34,680
to what we did with the adversarial examples,

1061
00:50:34,680 --> 00:50:37,460
but now, you're trying to find the model such

1062
00:50:37,460 --> 00:50:42,260
that-- in expectation over all of the possible distribution

1063
00:50:42,260 --> 00:50:45,620
shifts on a data set, you're still

1064
00:50:45,620 --> 00:50:48,860
going to minimize the loss.

1065
00:50:48,860 --> 00:50:51,580
And so you're basically, again, finding that worst case scenario

1066
00:50:51,580 --> 00:50:53,538
and trying to make sure that the model is still

1067
00:50:53,538 --> 00:50:59,010
robust to that for any given-- for any given distribution.

1068
00:50:59,010 --> 00:51:03,630
But again, this assumes that now you can only move the data

1069
00:51:03,630 --> 00:51:06,110
distribution a certain amount.

1070
00:51:06,110 --> 00:51:09,157
And measuring the difference in pixels is one thing,

1071
00:51:09,157 --> 00:51:10,990
but actually trying to think about measuring

1072
00:51:10,990 --> 00:51:13,110
the distance between probability distributions

1073
00:51:13,110 --> 00:51:14,210
can be quite complicated.

1074
00:51:14,210 --> 00:51:16,350
So there's Chi squared distance, people

1075
00:51:16,350 --> 00:51:17,990
use things like Wasserstein distance,

1076
00:51:17,990 --> 00:51:20,270
maximum mean discrepancy.

1077
00:51:20,270 --> 00:51:24,870
But how you actually measure how far away two distributions are,

1078
00:51:24,870 --> 00:51:28,470
if they're simple, like measurable distributions

1079
00:51:28,470 --> 00:51:30,373
in one dimension is one thing.

1080
00:51:30,373 --> 00:51:32,790
If you're trying to measure the difference in distribution

1081
00:51:32,790 --> 00:51:35,470
between 1 million image data set and another million image

1082
00:51:35,470 --> 00:51:37,285
data set, it gets a lot more complicated.

1083
00:51:37,285 --> 00:51:38,910
And a lot of times, what people will do

1084
00:51:38,910 --> 00:51:40,410
is they'll actually leverage learned

1085
00:51:40,410 --> 00:51:42,550
representations of those data sets and then try

1086
00:51:42,550 --> 00:51:45,030
to make some metric for the similarity

1087
00:51:45,030 --> 00:51:47,990
across, maybe, the clustering, the representation space.

1088
00:51:47,990 --> 00:51:50,590
This stuff is pretty tricky.

1089
00:51:50,590 --> 00:51:52,070
It's not definitely something that

1090
00:51:52,070 --> 00:51:55,880
works super well in practice.

1091
00:51:55,880 --> 00:51:59,240
So when you're actually trying to think about what this means

1092
00:51:59,240 --> 00:52:01,720
for generalization, essentially, DRO

1093
00:52:01,720 --> 00:52:05,400
optimizes for this set of possible training data sets

1094
00:52:05,400 --> 00:52:07,000
and distributions.

1095
00:52:07,000 --> 00:52:10,640
And so if you have some underlying data distribution, p,

1096
00:52:10,640 --> 00:52:12,920
and your empirical training data is

1097
00:52:12,920 --> 00:52:15,000
p sub n, so some subset of that or

1098
00:52:15,000 --> 00:52:17,880
some specific characterization of that,

1099
00:52:17,880 --> 00:52:20,820
then if you've built some distance metric

1100
00:52:20,820 --> 00:52:24,880
and you say that the new distribution of where you're

1101
00:52:24,880 --> 00:52:28,120
going to use the model is within some distance

1102
00:52:28,120 --> 00:52:31,960
of the true distribution, then you're

1103
00:52:31,960 --> 00:52:34,620
guaranteed to perform well on the true distribution.

1104
00:52:34,620 --> 00:52:36,222
So you're guaranteed to generalize.

1105
00:52:36,222 --> 00:52:37,680
But of course, I'm sure you can see

1106
00:52:37,680 --> 00:52:39,180
the challenge of the limitation here,

1107
00:52:39,180 --> 00:52:41,013
which is that you have to be able to measure

1108
00:52:41,013 --> 00:52:43,180
the distance between distributions,

1109
00:52:43,180 --> 00:52:44,760
and you have to be able to sample

1110
00:52:44,760 --> 00:52:47,462
these hypothetical distributions in a way that's realistic.

1111
00:52:47,462 --> 00:52:48,920
And of course, the more complicated

1112
00:52:48,920 --> 00:52:52,680
your data structures are, the harder that gets.

1113
00:52:52,680 --> 00:52:55,930
So interestingly, this has been applied pretty effectively when

1114
00:52:55,930 --> 00:52:58,450
you're talking about some of these class imbalance

1115
00:52:58,450 --> 00:53:00,050
challenges.

1116
00:53:00,050 --> 00:53:03,210
So here, you can use DRO.

1117
00:53:03,210 --> 00:53:06,250
So you assume your population has some number of subgroups.

1118
00:53:06,250 --> 00:53:08,770
Population here could be any given category.

1119
00:53:08,770 --> 00:53:10,330
So let's say the raccoons.

1120
00:53:10,330 --> 00:53:12,530
And now, maybe our raccoons have two subgroups,

1121
00:53:12,530 --> 00:53:16,738
like nighttime and daytime images of raccoons.

1122
00:53:16,738 --> 00:53:18,530
Traditionally, you would think about trying

1123
00:53:18,530 --> 00:53:21,810
to minimize the empirical risk or the average error

1124
00:53:21,810 --> 00:53:23,670
across these different subgroups,

1125
00:53:23,670 --> 00:53:26,730
assuming you have access to the actual labels

1126
00:53:26,730 --> 00:53:29,610
of those subgroups, which maybe for day versus night, we do.

1127
00:53:29,610 --> 00:53:33,730
Because we have a clock for our time or for our camera.

1128
00:53:33,730 --> 00:53:39,250
But it turns out that if you have a minority subgroup, if you

1129
00:53:39,250 --> 00:53:44,570
get 50% error on that minority group in terms of accuracy,

1130
00:53:44,570 --> 00:53:46,670
that's only 10% overall error.

1131
00:53:46,670 --> 00:53:48,930
So your model might learn, if it's effective,

1132
00:53:48,930 --> 00:53:51,990
to just ignore the minority group.

1133
00:53:51,990 --> 00:53:54,750
So you can ignore the minority group and still get decent loss.

1134
00:53:54,750 --> 00:53:58,990
And so the idea is that DRO can learn to effectively

1135
00:53:58,990 --> 00:54:02,790
automatically reweight your data to pay more attention

1136
00:54:02,790 --> 00:54:06,430
to those minority classes.

1137
00:54:06,430 --> 00:54:09,590
And so what that might actually look like is it's

1138
00:54:09,590 --> 00:54:12,110
forcing the model to place less emphasis

1139
00:54:12,110 --> 00:54:13,990
on noisy features and more emphasis

1140
00:54:13,990 --> 00:54:17,190
on useful and predictive features.

1141
00:54:17,190 --> 00:54:20,590
And it also seems to be explicitly, empirically,

1142
00:54:20,590 --> 00:54:23,170
at least, shifting the distribution

1143
00:54:23,170 --> 00:54:25,910
so that they're more separable.

1144
00:54:25,910 --> 00:54:29,410
But there's some really nice work, actually,

1145
00:54:29,410 --> 00:54:33,710
that shows that you can do this, and there's adaptations of DRO

1146
00:54:33,710 --> 00:54:36,590
to do this, even if you don't know what the subgroups are

1147
00:54:36,590 --> 00:54:38,950
or how many subgroups there are.

1148
00:54:38,950 --> 00:54:42,150
So people use DRO a lot for some of these fairness

1149
00:54:42,150 --> 00:54:43,090
type of problems.

1150
00:54:43,090 --> 00:54:45,893
You want a model to learn to recognize doctors,

1151
00:54:45,893 --> 00:54:47,310
and you don't want it to be biased

1152
00:54:47,310 --> 00:54:49,710
against female doctors or Black doctors,

1153
00:54:49,710 --> 00:54:53,160
even though the training data is biased against those groups.

1154
00:54:53,160 --> 00:54:56,220
And so in that worst case distribution shift,

1155
00:54:56,220 --> 00:54:58,895
you could imagine, you might only sample those minority

1156
00:54:58,895 --> 00:55:00,520
groups, and then your model performance

1157
00:55:00,520 --> 00:55:03,960
would be significantly worse if you aren't making sure

1158
00:55:03,960 --> 00:55:06,040
that you're robust to that.

1159
00:55:06,040 --> 00:55:08,400
So what do we do about distribution shift?

1160
00:55:08,400 --> 00:55:11,160
First, you can try distributionally robust

1161
00:55:11,160 --> 00:55:13,960
optimization, or there's many other ways

1162
00:55:13,960 --> 00:55:16,980
to think about building robustness, extensions of this,

1163
00:55:16,980 --> 00:55:21,280
cetera, that take an algorithmic approach to trying to reduce--

1164
00:55:21,280 --> 00:55:24,560
to trying to make models more robust to these types

1165
00:55:24,560 --> 00:55:27,720
of distribution shifts or make them more fair.

1166
00:55:27,720 --> 00:55:31,400
You can also do things explicitly learn

1167
00:55:31,400 --> 00:55:34,620
priors over the distribution shifts that you see.

1168
00:55:34,620 --> 00:55:38,440
So, for example, subpopulation shift.

1169
00:55:38,440 --> 00:55:42,880
So this is a case-- this is an example by [INAUDIBLE] where

1170
00:55:42,880 --> 00:55:45,180
they looked at species identification,

1171
00:55:45,180 --> 00:55:47,760
and they explicitly learned separately

1172
00:55:47,760 --> 00:55:53,130
a modular, like, spatiotemporal prior over places and times

1173
00:55:53,130 --> 00:55:56,490
that then they use to remap the likelihoods of any given

1174
00:55:56,490 --> 00:55:59,130
probability or any given species based

1175
00:55:59,130 --> 00:56:00,790
on where the image was taken.

1176
00:56:00,790 --> 00:56:01,470
Makes sense.

1177
00:56:01,470 --> 00:56:03,770
This is what ecologists do.

1178
00:56:03,770 --> 00:56:06,890
Often, there are categories that look really similar.

1179
00:56:06,890 --> 00:56:13,232
And if you just saw the image without any context about where,

1180
00:56:13,232 --> 00:56:14,690
when it was taken, it might be hard

1181
00:56:14,690 --> 00:56:16,930
even for an expert to be able to distinguish them.

1182
00:56:16,930 --> 00:56:21,210
But if you have that additional information, it's quite useful.

1183
00:56:21,210 --> 00:56:24,850
And so this is just addressing this generalization problem

1184
00:56:24,850 --> 00:56:29,050
as an image categorization with this kind of specialization

1185
00:56:29,050 --> 00:56:32,690
that's coming from a prior based on space and time.

1186
00:56:32,690 --> 00:56:35,250
So you can think about capturing some of the information you

1187
00:56:35,250 --> 00:56:37,410
might have about the structure of your domain

1188
00:56:37,410 --> 00:56:41,422
or the distributions spatially, temporally, et cetera,

1189
00:56:41,422 --> 00:56:43,130
within your domain, and actually building

1190
00:56:43,130 --> 00:56:46,570
that directly into your model as a way to do a better job.

1191
00:56:46,570 --> 00:56:48,410
And those models improve species accuracy

1192
00:56:48,410 --> 00:56:51,580
by like, 10% on a naturalist.

1193
00:56:51,580 --> 00:56:55,500
You can think about mechanisms like domain adaptation.

1194
00:56:55,500 --> 00:56:58,940
So this is actually an entire category

1195
00:56:58,940 --> 00:57:03,380
of models that tries to learn given a source

1196
00:57:03,380 --> 00:57:06,940
domain and a target domain where maybe you don't necessarily

1197
00:57:06,940 --> 00:57:08,840
have labels for your target domain,

1198
00:57:08,840 --> 00:57:11,760
how to align their representations during training

1199
00:57:11,760 --> 00:57:13,660
so that you can enforce generalization

1200
00:57:13,660 --> 00:57:15,360
or adaptation to a new target domain,

1201
00:57:15,360 --> 00:57:17,900
even if you don't have a lot of data or labeled data.

1202
00:57:17,900 --> 00:57:22,020
I'll talk a little bit more about that in next lecture.

1203
00:57:22,020 --> 00:57:23,200
This is pretty interesting.

1204
00:57:23,200 --> 00:57:24,620
You can come up with creative ways

1205
00:57:24,620 --> 00:57:28,083
to try to diagnose the failure modes of your model, which often

1206
00:57:28,083 --> 00:57:30,500
teaches you more about the biases in your initial training

1207
00:57:30,500 --> 00:57:33,180
data than anything else, but they can be really interesting.

1208
00:57:33,180 --> 00:57:35,740
And so there's ways to think about doing that by just

1209
00:57:35,740 --> 00:57:37,660
striving your evaluation.

1210
00:57:37,660 --> 00:57:40,960
So breaking down evaluation per category,

1211
00:57:40,960 --> 00:57:43,140
along different dimensions, like night versus day,

1212
00:57:43,140 --> 00:57:46,120
or different sexes of the participants,

1213
00:57:46,120 --> 00:57:48,400
assuming you have the labels to be able to do that,

1214
00:57:48,400 --> 00:57:50,560
break that down and look at the relative

1215
00:57:50,560 --> 00:57:54,600
maybe call it fairness across the category set.

1216
00:57:54,600 --> 00:57:57,320
This is an interesting example coming from Aleksander Madry's

1217
00:57:57,320 --> 00:58:01,160
group, where they actually use modern generative AI

1218
00:58:01,160 --> 00:58:06,200
models to generate hypothetical counterfactuals to try

1219
00:58:06,200 --> 00:58:11,840
to understand or diagnose failure modes in training.

1220
00:58:11,840 --> 00:58:14,655
And so here, for example, what they found--

1221
00:58:14,655 --> 00:58:16,280
one interesting example that they found

1222
00:58:16,280 --> 00:58:18,197
through this diagnosis, and then they actually

1223
00:58:18,197 --> 00:58:20,280
were able to demonstrate it on real data,

1224
00:58:20,280 --> 00:58:22,960
is that these models are really good at recognizing plates

1225
00:58:22,960 --> 00:58:25,700
if they have food on them, but if it doesn't have food on it,

1226
00:58:25,700 --> 00:58:27,480
it's consistently being categorized

1227
00:58:27,480 --> 00:58:30,640
as like, trays or buckets.

1228
00:58:30,640 --> 00:58:32,780
So you can try to diagnose the failures,

1229
00:58:32,780 --> 00:58:34,200
and that might be so that you can

1230
00:58:34,200 --> 00:58:35,960
try to then collect training data to be

1231
00:58:35,960 --> 00:58:37,260
robust to those failures.

1232
00:58:37,260 --> 00:58:41,000
It also can just be useful if you're trying to think about how

1233
00:58:41,000 --> 00:58:43,820
and in what way you should trust a model in deployment.

1234
00:58:47,160 --> 00:58:50,170
I would argue that probably the best

1235
00:58:50,170 --> 00:58:52,930
thing you can do in any of these cases

1236
00:58:52,930 --> 00:58:55,890
is just get training data that's representative of your test

1237
00:58:55,890 --> 00:58:56,770
domain.

1238
00:58:56,770 --> 00:59:00,370
So in practice, a lot of these algorithmic approaches,

1239
00:59:00,370 --> 00:59:05,530
these creative ways to try to train these models to be robust,

1240
00:59:05,530 --> 00:59:07,410
to be effective, usually, they're

1241
00:59:07,410 --> 00:59:09,970
just significantly worse than just more training

1242
00:59:09,970 --> 00:59:12,410
data that's representative of your test domain.

1243
00:59:12,410 --> 00:59:16,050
So from a practical perspective, if you really need something

1244
00:59:16,050 --> 00:59:19,290
to work well, you have to invest in the right kind of training

1245
00:59:19,290 --> 00:59:20,130
data.

1246
00:59:20,130 --> 00:59:23,130
And if that's something that you just cannot get for various

1247
00:59:23,130 --> 00:59:26,090
reasons, various constraints, maybe the species is super

1248
00:59:26,090 --> 00:59:30,410
endangered, or you're trying to get a model to work on a planet

1249
00:59:30,410 --> 00:59:33,970
that we've never landed something on, or we're trying

1250
00:59:33,970 --> 00:59:37,850
to get models to work under the Arctic or in the deep sea where

1251
00:59:37,850 --> 00:59:41,970
we just are not actually able to collect large-scale training

1252
00:59:41,970 --> 00:59:45,410
data, then you're probably going to have to have a human look

1253
00:59:45,410 --> 00:59:47,940
at a lot of the data still.

1254
00:59:47,940 --> 00:59:49,980
This kind of where do we actually

1255
00:59:49,980 --> 00:59:52,460
see these things work in practice,

1256
00:59:52,460 --> 00:59:55,660
often, it's really just where we have in-distribution training

1257
00:59:55,660 --> 00:59:56,700
data.

1258
00:59:56,700 --> 00:59:59,980
And so this is a really active area of research.

1259
00:59:59,980 --> 01:00:01,448
And for any of you that are working

1260
01:00:01,448 --> 01:00:03,740
on trying to bring deep learning to your own scientific

1261
01:00:03,740 --> 01:00:07,300
challenges or problems, I would totally recommend that you look

1262
01:00:07,300 --> 01:00:09,080
into this literature when you think about,

1263
01:00:09,080 --> 01:00:11,540
how do I get the model to work where I want it to work?

1264
01:00:11,540 --> 01:00:14,880
But I also think that if you have limited time and effort,

1265
01:00:14,880 --> 01:00:17,820
sometimes, it's just worth it to label more data.

1266
01:00:17,820 --> 01:00:20,500
At least, practically, that's what I've found.

1267
01:00:20,500 --> 01:00:22,220
Cool.

1268
01:00:22,220 --> 01:00:26,500
So, in summary, out-of-distribution

1269
01:00:26,500 --> 01:00:28,160
generalization is a big challenge.

1270
01:00:28,160 --> 01:00:30,580
It's still a big challenge.

1271
01:00:30,580 --> 01:00:32,740
But digging into it helps us understand

1272
01:00:32,740 --> 01:00:34,940
what neural networks learn, and it also really helps

1273
01:00:34,940 --> 01:00:38,780
us try to understand this very tight and complex relationship

1274
01:00:38,780 --> 01:00:41,580
between the training data and where we can effectively

1275
01:00:41,580 --> 01:00:42,820
use models.

1276
01:00:42,820 --> 01:00:45,230
And then we talked about adversarial examples

1277
01:00:45,230 --> 01:00:47,450
and distribution shifts today.

1278
01:00:47,450 --> 01:00:51,230
Are there any other questions?

1279
01:00:51,230 --> 01:00:51,910
Yeah?

1280
01:00:51,910 --> 01:00:54,350
AUDIENCE: Has anyone looked at activation functions?

1281
01:00:54,350 --> 01:00:57,870
Like, do activation functions change how easy or difficult

1282
01:00:57,870 --> 01:01:02,030
it is to fool one of these?

1283
01:01:02,030 --> 01:01:05,070
SARA BEERY: So you mean like things like Grad-CAM?

1284
01:01:05,070 --> 01:01:06,470
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

1285
01:01:06,470 --> 01:01:07,670
SARA BEERY: Oh.

1286
01:01:07,670 --> 01:01:09,470
AUDIENCE: There's a stark non-linearity

1287
01:01:09,470 --> 01:01:11,590
between [INAUDIBLE] it's not.

1288
01:01:11,590 --> 01:01:13,150
SARA BEERY: Gotcha.

1289
01:01:13,150 --> 01:01:15,790
The question was, have people explored

1290
01:01:15,790 --> 01:01:18,590
how the choice of activation functions, so something

1291
01:01:18,590 --> 01:01:20,970
like a ReLU or a GELU Or something,

1292
01:01:20,970 --> 01:01:25,750
how that affects generalization or robustness.

1293
01:01:25,750 --> 01:01:30,270
I bet, but I don't know any literature on it.

1294
01:01:30,270 --> 01:01:36,220
I think my intuition says it wouldn't have a huge effect.

1295
01:01:36,220 --> 01:01:38,470
But it would be interesting to dig in and try and see.

1296
01:01:38,470 --> 01:01:40,512
And it might have a bigger effect on simpler data

1297
01:01:40,512 --> 01:01:41,773
sets than more complex ones.

1298
01:01:41,773 --> 01:01:43,190
So there might be papers that show

1299
01:01:43,190 --> 01:01:46,520
that has an effect on MNIST style training or CIFAR style

1300
01:01:46,520 --> 01:01:49,895
training, but that might not actually generalize versus all

1301
01:01:49,895 --> 01:01:51,520
of the other different complicated ways

1302
01:01:51,520 --> 01:01:55,560
that models and architectures affect performance.

1303
01:01:55,560 --> 01:01:59,040
But that's totally just my best guess.

1304
01:01:59,040 --> 01:02:00,300
I'm actually kind of curious.

